354
00:13:02.485 --> 00:13:05.405
That's warmer than the Pacific this past weekend.

355
00:13:05.405 --> 00:13:06.449
Yeah, yeah, definitely.

356
00:13:06.449 --> 00:13:08.096
And it was, there was a chop,

357
00:13:08.096 --> 00:13:10.096
there was a crosscurrent

358
00:13:10.942 --> 00:13:12.462
that made it a little more challenging,

359
00:13:12.462 --> 00:13:13.809
and then sighting is more challenging.

360
00:13:13.809 --> 00:13:15.635
I'm used to swimming along the coast.

361
00:13:15.635 --> 00:13:18.343
So crossing even a small channel like that.

362
00:13:18.343 --> 00:13:19.601
Do they have buoys up?

363
00:13:19.601 --> 00:13:21.028
They had kayakers.

364
00:13:21.028 --> 00:13:24.290
And so we were, like, I was with my buddy, John Moore,

365
00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:27.043
who I swim with out here and he and I both said,

366
00:13:27.043 --> 00:13:30.344
let's go out 'cause Antonio told us,

367
00:13:30.344 --> 00:13:32.458
"Go a little bit further to the right,

368
00:13:32.458 --> 00:13:34.087
"and then you'll catch the current."

369
00:13:34.087 --> 00:13:35.238
Towards the bridge.

370
00:13:35.238 --> 00:13:37.131
Toward, yeah, "Go a little bit towards the bridge

371
00:13:37.131 --> 00:13:38.269
"and then catch the current."

372
00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:39.213
And we thought we had,

373
00:13:39.213 --> 00:13:40.430
we went a little too far out.

374
00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:42.870
And then we caught the current, and which was good,

375
00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:44.492
but by the time we got to Aquatic Park,

376
00:13:44.492 --> 00:13:47.839
I thought I was, I just kept going the wrong way.

377
00:13:47.839 --> 00:13:51.203
Many times I thought the finish line was on the far side,

378
00:13:51.203 --> 00:13:54.409
it turned out is on the near side, and I screwed it up.

379
00:13:54.409 --> 00:13:57.909
So the bottom line is I've done a 5K swim,

380
00:13:59.654 --> 00:14:04.010
I've done Alcatraz and I've done a four-mile swim once

381
00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.395
in The Bahamas ages ago.

382
00:14:06.395 --> 00:14:08.438
And each time I came near the end,

383
00:14:08.438 --> 00:14:11.044
never the last, but very close to last,

384
00:14:11.044 --> 00:14:13.566
this time at least I was in the middle of the pack.

385
00:14:13.566 --> 00:14:14.442
Aha.

386
00:14:14.442 --> 00:14:16.376
So like there is that, I did better.

387
00:14:16.376 --> 00:14:17.252
That's huge.

388
00:14:17.252 --> 00:14:18.128
Yeah.

389
00:14:18.128 --> 00:14:20.693
But also I didn't meet my goal time

390
00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:22.040
and I am slightly annoyed,

391
00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:24.540
which means I have to do Alcatraz again.

392
00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:25.415
Right.

393
00:14:25.415 --> 00:14:27.173
But if you zoom out and you can see this arc

394
00:14:27.173 --> 00:14:30.090
in your trajectory, that's typical.

395
00:14:30.995 --> 00:14:34.890
And I think that that was wise advice to tack to your right,

396
00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:38.253
because we talked about this before, like the longer,

397
00:14:38.253 --> 00:14:41.835
you start in between the ebb and the slack, right?

398
00:14:41.835 --> 00:14:44.289
Like so there's this kind of dead zone

399
00:14:44.289 --> 00:14:45.483
that is very temporary,

400
00:14:45.483 --> 00:14:49.065
but quickly the tide is gonna start coming in

401
00:14:49.065 --> 00:14:51.095
and you know that you're gonna get pushed to your left.

402
00:14:51.095 --> 00:14:53.251
So it is wise to tack to your right.

403
00:14:53.251 --> 00:14:57.175
The idea is that in that tacking, then as the tide picks up,

404
00:14:57.175 --> 00:14:59.570
you're gonna get pushed to your left,

405
00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:02.754
and ultimately what you're gonna see is a straight line,

406
00:15:02.754 --> 00:15:04.338
not quite as arc does that, but.

407
00:15:04.338 --> 00:15:06.185
It's not even really arcs.

408
00:15:06.185 --> 00:15:07.060
It's more jagged.

