482
00:17:37.098 --> 00:17:39.840
We'll get into more about the Malibu Triathlon

483
00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:40.716
in a minute, but-

484
00:17:40.716 --> 00:17:42.767
Bit that's over a half.

485
00:17:42.767 --> 00:17:44.515
It was supposed to be a half-mile swim and you swam

486
00:17:44.515 --> 00:17:46.357
a couple hundred yards too long as well.

487
00:17:46.357 --> 00:17:48.296
Well, yeah, it's true, it says 1,200.

488
00:17:48.296 --> 00:17:49.756
I thought maybe the swim longer

489
00:17:49.756 --> 00:17:50.837
'cause it was supposed to be,

490
00:17:50.837 --> 00:17:55.129
I thought it was like a 700-meter swim, which, well,

491
00:17:55.129 --> 00:17:56.985
with the running part, it got added to it

492
00:17:56.985 --> 00:17:58.953
'cause I didn't turn the garment off

493
00:17:58.953 --> 00:18:00.168
until I was in the transition zone.

494
00:18:00.168 --> 00:18:02.192
So there was a good, I don't know,

495
00:18:02.192 --> 00:18:04.347
more than a quarter mile of running.

496
00:18:04.347 --> 00:18:05.220
That's a hell of a pace, dude.

497
00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:08.531
I don't wanna spoil it, you tell the story,

498
00:18:08.531 --> 00:18:10.048
but you really kicked ass.

499
00:18:10.048 --> 00:18:11.863
Well, look, we had fun.

500
00:18:11.863 --> 00:18:13.416
So here's how it came about.

501
00:18:13.416 --> 00:18:17.166
Alexi Pappas, podcast-favorite, contacted me.

502
00:18:18.651 --> 00:18:23.035
She said that she'd been contacted by the Malibu Triathlon

503
00:18:23.035 --> 00:18:26.681
and invited to do the celebrity race as a relay.

504
00:18:26.681 --> 00:18:29.153
She wanted to know if I would do it with her,

505
00:18:29.153 --> 00:18:31.565
which of course that sounds like fun,

506
00:18:31.565 --> 00:18:33.222
and she got her bestie,

507
00:18:33.222 --> 00:18:36.133
Mary Cain involved, also friend of the pod.

508
00:18:36.133 --> 00:18:38.966
So this is a pod-tastic relay team

509
00:18:40.783 --> 00:18:41.978
that we put together.

510
00:18:41.978 --> 00:18:43.805
Mary, Mary a runner?

511
00:18:43.805 --> 00:18:46.797
Mary, for those that don't know,

512
00:18:46.797 --> 00:18:49.766
was the greatest runner of her generation as a young person.

513
00:18:49.766 --> 00:18:52.900
Her career didn't really pan out as she would've liked.

514
00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:57.850
She had experiences training under Alberto Salazar,

515
00:18:57.850 --> 00:18:59.065
the Nike Oregon Project

516
00:18:59.065 --> 00:19:01.390
that kind of derailed expectations

517
00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:03.683
around her becoming this great Olympic champion.

518
00:19:03.683 --> 00:19:06.276
She has now emerged from that phase of her life

519
00:19:06.276 --> 00:19:08.014
and started this running team

520
00:19:08.014 --> 00:19:11.264
called Atalanta in New York City,

521
00:19:11.264 --> 00:19:15.930
that's all about women empowerment, athlete empowerment.

522
00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:17.845
And she's been spending time on the bike.

523
00:19:17.845 --> 00:19:20.576
So she doesn't have any experience racing bikes

524
00:19:20.576 --> 00:19:24.010
or doing triathlons, but she was doing the bike leg,

525
00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:27.778
Alexi the run leg, and I agreed to do this one leg,

526
00:19:27.778 --> 00:19:29.532
and I thought, this is gonna be great,

527
00:19:29.532 --> 00:19:32.617
first of all, crazy that we would be invited to do

528
00:19:32.617 --> 00:19:34.444
the celebrity division of this race

529
00:19:34.444 --> 00:19:36.901
'cause it's like, are they hard up

530
00:19:36.901 --> 00:19:38.922
for like, like traditionally over the years,

531
00:19:38.922 --> 00:19:42.011
like the Malibu Triathlon is like an institution in Malibu

532
00:19:42.011 --> 00:19:43.174
and it's this celebration,

533
00:19:43.174 --> 00:19:45.747
not only of multi-sport but of our community,

534
00:19:45.747 --> 00:19:49.816
and it's really the only multi-sport race that I'm aware of

535
00:19:49.816 --> 00:19:51.192
where there's this intersection

536
00:19:51.192 --> 00:19:55.074
between the entertainment industry, like Hollywood,

537
00:19:55.074 --> 00:19:57.420
with something athletic.

538
00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:59.086
And over the years it's been graced

539
00:19:59.086 --> 00:20:03.771
by many an A-list movie star, like J-Lo did it.

540
00:20:03.771 --> 00:20:05.147
I remember when she did it.

541
00:20:05.147 --> 00:20:06.574
Matthew McConaughey has done it.

542
00:20:06.574 --> 00:20:08.041
Like a lot of people that you know

543
00:20:08.041 --> 00:20:10.420
from movies and television participate in this,

544
00:20:10.420 --> 00:20:13.264
they raised tons of money for children's hospital,

545
00:20:13.264 --> 00:20:15.816
I believe, and various charities over the years.

546
00:20:15.816 --> 00:20:18.298
So I thought this will be hilarious, let's do it.

547
00:20:18.298 --> 00:20:19.979
We're actually all athletes.

548
00:20:19.979 --> 00:20:23.070
I wouldn't consider us celebrities in the traditional sense,

549
00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.910
but we should just destroy this

550
00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.707
because like we're gonna be competing against people

551
00:20:27.707 --> 00:20:31.257
on reality TV shows and like sitcom actors and people

552
00:20:31.257 --> 00:20:35.840
on CBS shows or something, like this is gonna be great.

553
00:20:36.727 --> 00:20:38.096
You're gonna compete against.

554
00:20:38.096 --> 00:20:40.429
And the guys from "Suits,"

555
00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:43.078
(Adam and Rich laughing)

556
00:20:43.078 --> 00:20:45.646
and stuff like that, right?

557
00:20:45.646 --> 00:20:47.365
'Cause that's the way it's always been.

558
00:20:47.365 --> 00:20:48.220
Right.

559
00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:51.955
And what was really fun and fantastic and surprising

560
00:20:51.955 --> 00:20:55.321
and amazing and also like tricky about the whole thing

561
00:20:55.321 --> 00:20:56.930
is we show up and it turns out

562
00:20:56.930 --> 00:21:00.917
that there are a couple people that you know

563
00:21:00.917 --> 00:21:02.879
from, like Chace Crawford,

564
00:21:02.879 --> 00:21:04.691
like people who are in movies and television,

565
00:21:04.691 --> 00:21:08.095
there was like five or six of those types of people.

566
00:21:08.095 --> 00:21:09.668
But for the most part,

567
00:21:09.668 --> 00:21:13.916
all the relay teams in the celebrity division were stacked

568
00:21:13.916 --> 00:21:17.193
with professional triathletes and Olympians.

569
00:21:17.193 --> 00:21:20.386
Like basically, all of the people who medalled (laughs)

570
00:21:20.386 --> 00:21:23.499
in the Tokyo Olympics in triathlon,

571
00:21:23.499 --> 00:21:27.043
were all like on various relay teams.

572
00:21:27.043 --> 00:21:29.933
So way more often than not,

573
00:21:29.933 --> 00:21:33.751
like these teams were propagated by legit,

574
00:21:33.751 --> 00:21:37.597
like elite athletes in their prime triathletes

575
00:21:37.597 --> 00:21:41.024
in their prime, and not reality TV stars.

576
00:21:41.024 --> 00:21:45.047
And part of that is because, just for some context,

577
00:21:45.047 --> 00:21:47.438
an organization called Super League Triathlon,

578
00:21:47.438 --> 00:21:51.372
which is this new race series in triathlon that's oriented

579
00:21:51.372 --> 00:21:54.914
around like really good prize money for the triathletes

580
00:21:54.914 --> 00:21:58.024
as well as trying to get audience excitement

581
00:21:58.024 --> 00:22:00.910
around triathlon because they reimagined the format

582
00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:04.361
to make it much more washable and exciting for the viewer.

583
00:22:04.361 --> 00:22:06.056
And it's a big deal in Europe.

584
00:22:06.056 --> 00:22:08.523
They do all these races as part of a circuit.

585
00:22:08.523 --> 00:22:10.045
They accumulate points.

586
00:22:10.045 --> 00:22:11.245
Well, Super League Triathlon,

587
00:22:11.245 --> 00:22:13.830
the organization, purchased the Malibu Triathlon,

588
00:22:13.830 --> 00:22:15.169
so they now own it,

589
00:22:15.169 --> 00:22:19.002
and this was the first North American instance

590
00:22:20.604 --> 00:22:22.438
of Super League Triathlon

591
00:22:22.438 --> 00:22:25.357
being conducted in this part of the world.

592
00:22:25.357 --> 00:22:29.105
So on Saturday, that was the, all the pros raced,

593
00:22:29.105 --> 00:22:32.322
and you have like the Olympic champion, Christian Blue,

594
00:22:32.322 --> 00:22:34.278
you had Lucy Charles-Barclay,

595
00:22:34.278 --> 00:22:36.711
who just won the 70.3 World Championships

596
00:22:36.711 --> 00:22:38.017
a couple of weeks ago,

597
00:22:38.017 --> 00:22:40.918
like all of these super elite young people.

598
00:22:40.918 --> 00:22:43.891
And then they just took all of those incredible athletes

599
00:22:43.891 --> 00:22:45.864
and said, we'll put you guys on relays

600
00:22:45.864 --> 00:22:47.319
in the celebrity division.

601
00:22:47.319 --> 00:22:51.101
So a lot of them had like two Olympians or like-

602
00:22:51.101 --> 00:22:51.970
Crazy.

603
00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:53.661
And then they'll have one person who's kind

604
00:22:53.661 --> 00:22:55.372
of like the Achilles Heel,

605
00:22:55.372 --> 00:22:56.637
like Hollywood person or whatever.

606
00:22:56.637 --> 00:23:01.637
So that changed the sort of complexion (laughs)

607
00:23:02.153 --> 00:23:03.284
of this whole thing,

608
00:23:03.284 --> 00:23:04.821
because we're going and going and thinking,

609
00:23:04.821 --> 00:23:06.670
we're gonna destroy this thing.

610
00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:09.475
Like there's nobody who's gonna come anywhere near to us.

611
00:23:09.475 --> 00:23:12.322
And that was a big catalyst for getting me back

612
00:23:12.322 --> 00:23:13.171
into the pool

613
00:23:13.171 --> 00:23:15.732
and like training with some intentionality around it.

614
00:23:15.732 --> 00:23:17.330
So not that I'm in any kind

615
00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:19.668
of race fit situation right now,

616
00:23:19.668 --> 00:23:21.632
but at least I've been mixing it up in the pool

617
00:23:21.632 --> 00:23:25.599
and felt comfortable kind of getting after it.

618
00:23:25.599 --> 00:23:28.017
So we show up at the transition area in the morning,

619
00:23:28.017 --> 00:23:30.863
and it's like, we're backing our bike right next

620
00:23:30.863 --> 00:23:34.079
to Lucy Charles Barkley, ad then there's Christian Blue.

621
00:23:34.079 --> 00:23:34.995
No relation, no relation?

622
00:23:34.995 --> 00:23:37.625
Yeah, no relation to the Charles Barkley from the NBA.

623
00:23:37.625 --> 00:23:38.520
Yeah.

624
00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.130
But like a superstar in triathlon.

625
00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:45.584
And I had met Christian Blue a couple nights prior

626
00:23:45.584 --> 00:23:46.454
at an event.

627
00:23:46.454 --> 00:23:48.091
And then there he is,

628
00:23:48.091 --> 00:23:51.249
and he had another Tokyo Olympian doing the swim

629
00:23:51.249 --> 00:23:54.470
on his relay, like, and he was the transition area felt

630
00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:55.966
like being in the transition area

631
00:23:55.966 --> 00:23:58.672
of like the elite performers in all of the sport.

632
00:23:58.672 --> 00:23:59.623
(Rich laughs)

633
00:23:59.623 --> 00:24:00.475
That's crazy.

634
00:24:00.475 --> 00:24:01.324
Which was great.

635
00:24:01.324 --> 00:24:03.223
So I line up for the swim,

636
00:24:03.223 --> 00:24:07.056
I'm doing the swim leg, and I'm looking around

637
00:24:08.497 --> 00:24:12.747
and I'm not seeing a lot of like elite-type people.

638
00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:17.209
So I thought, mm-hmm, maybe I got this thing sorted out.

639
00:24:17.209 --> 00:24:19.848
Like I think I'm in good shape, and they're counting down.

640
00:24:19.848 --> 00:24:22.316
They're like the two-minute warning, the one-minute warning.

641
00:24:22.316 --> 00:24:24.887
And then with like 30 seconds to go before the gun

642
00:24:24.887 --> 00:24:26.803
was gonna go off for our heat,

643
00:24:26.803 --> 00:24:31.401
like five or six athletes suddenly like run up

644
00:24:31.401 --> 00:24:33.796
from the water and like join our group.

645
00:24:33.796 --> 00:24:36.882
Like the real triathletes I've been in the water

646
00:24:36.882 --> 00:24:38.863
warming up, and they shoved the last minute.

647
00:24:38.863 --> 00:24:40.381
And I was like, oh, nevermind.

648
00:24:40.381 --> 00:24:42.054
Were you in the front?

649
00:24:42.054 --> 00:24:42.934
Did you like know?

650
00:24:42.934 --> 00:24:45.299
Yeah, I was like, yeah, I'm like looking, where's my line.

651
00:24:45.299 --> 00:24:46.341
Like where do I line up?

652
00:24:46.341 --> 00:24:51.167
And like, yeah, I'm very like, I'm all about that.

653
00:24:51.167 --> 00:24:53.492
So my aspirations for like winning

654
00:24:53.492 --> 00:24:55.516
my heat of the swim went out the window.

655
00:24:55.516 --> 00:24:56.901
So we run in-

656
00:24:56.901 --> 00:24:58.225
Did you get wet first at all?

657
00:24:58.225 --> 00:24:59.144
You're dry?

658
00:24:59.144 --> 00:25:00.648
Yeah. It was, I should've.

659
00:25:00.648 --> 00:25:01.500
I should've actually.

660
00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:02.654
Does that help getting a little bit first?

661
00:25:02.654 --> 00:25:03.972
Yeah, of course, you should warm up.

662
00:25:03.972 --> 00:25:05.087
I don't know why I didn't.

663
00:25:05.087 --> 00:25:06.052
That was dumb.

664
00:25:06.052 --> 00:25:08.431
But anyway, we run into the water,

665
00:25:08.431 --> 00:25:11.488
and to the first buoy, I was right up,

666
00:25:11.488 --> 00:25:13.793
I was in, I was sort of on the heels.

667
00:25:13.793 --> 00:25:15.951
I was in the lead pack,

668
00:25:15.951 --> 00:25:18.344
but kind of in the back part of the lead pack

669
00:25:18.344 --> 00:25:20.278
of like five or six people.

670
00:25:20.278 --> 00:25:24.327
And we round the first buoy and had alongside the shore

671
00:25:24.327 --> 00:25:27.077
and I lost touch with that group,

672
00:25:28.121 --> 00:25:32.520
I fell off the back, and I thought, oh man,

673
00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:37.263
like I thought I was like a better swimmer than maybe I am,

674
00:25:37.263 --> 00:25:39.932
like, I need to like reevaluate.

675
00:25:39.932 --> 00:25:41.131
Reevaluating your life.

676
00:25:41.131 --> 00:25:43.617
Yeah, I'm like, I need to rethink

677
00:25:43.617 --> 00:25:45.493
like, 'cause I was like,

678
00:25:45.493 --> 00:25:47.386
I'm a pretty good swimmer, like of people my age,

679
00:25:47.386 --> 00:25:48.770
there aren't very many people my age

680
00:25:48.770 --> 00:25:51.175
that can swim as well.

681
00:25:51.175 --> 00:25:54.230
And, so I thought, I could be competitive with any

682
00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:55.081
of these people.

683
00:25:55.081 --> 00:25:56.169
And when I fell off the back of that group,

684
00:25:56.169 --> 00:25:59.138
I literally was like, yeah, I was examining my life.

685
00:25:59.138 --> 00:26:01.061
I thought, you know what?

686
00:26:01.061 --> 00:26:02.917
You've really indulged your ego here.

687
00:26:02.917 --> 00:26:05.353
Like, you're really not all that, dude.

688
00:26:05.353 --> 00:26:08.782
Like you need to really recalibrate and you need

689
00:26:08.782 --> 00:26:11.844
a lot more training and like I'm running this whole thing

690
00:26:11.844 --> 00:26:13.065
in my head the whole time.

691
00:26:13.065 --> 00:26:14.867
You're like, how dare you, Rich?

692
00:26:14.867 --> 00:26:15.723
How dare you?

693
00:26:15.723 --> 00:26:17.134
And then I get out,

694
00:26:17.134 --> 00:26:19.221
we run up the shore and the thing is like,

695
00:26:19.221 --> 00:26:20.662
there's a bunch of like,

696
00:26:20.662 --> 00:26:21.745
we were the fourth wave or something like that.

697
00:26:21.745 --> 00:26:25.093
So you start catching up to the waves

698
00:26:25.093 --> 00:26:27.073
before the slower people in the waves before you,

699
00:26:27.073 --> 00:26:28.829
and then it becomes like this obstacle course

700
00:26:28.829 --> 00:26:31.034
of just like swimming around all of these people,

701
00:26:31.034 --> 00:26:31.883
run up on shore,

702
00:26:31.883 --> 00:26:33.807
have a much longer run to the transition

703
00:26:33.807 --> 00:26:34.744
than I would've preferred.

704
00:26:34.744 --> 00:26:36.785
That was actually the hardest part of the whole thing.

705
00:26:36.785 --> 00:26:37.998
Is that why you were tumbled over-

706
00:26:37.998 --> 00:26:39.539
I was tired.

707
00:26:39.539 --> 00:26:42.967
The run was really the thing that like blew me out, I had-

708
00:26:42.967 --> 00:26:43.917
Because when you get out of the water

709
00:26:43.917 --> 00:26:45.450
and, is grueling.

710
00:26:45.450 --> 00:26:47.949
That, but then you run up and then you're on pavement.

711
00:26:47.949 --> 00:26:50.342
They have like this carpet on this pavement to take it.

712
00:26:50.342 --> 00:26:52.842
And the transition area

713
00:26:52.842 --> 00:26:55.178
for like the, quote, unquote, celebrity division

714
00:26:55.178 --> 00:26:56.529
was all the way at the NCA

715
00:26:56.529 --> 00:26:57.875
to run through the entire transition area

716
00:26:57.875 --> 00:27:01.383
for all of the participants to get to the very, very end.

717
00:27:01.383 --> 00:27:03.446
So, yeah, I was definitely fatigued,

718
00:27:03.446 --> 00:27:05.836
and I was beating myself up, oh God,

719
00:27:05.836 --> 00:27:06.857
I thought I was gonna be better.

720
00:27:06.857 --> 00:27:09.373
I was gonna be out with the lead group.

721
00:27:09.373 --> 00:27:10.633
And they were like, no, you were fine.

722
00:27:10.633 --> 00:27:14.315
Everybody who beat you was a professional.

723
00:27:14.315 --> 00:27:15.189
Certainly I felt better.

724
00:27:15.189 --> 00:27:16.614
(Rich and Adam laughing)

725
00:27:16.614 --> 00:27:17.465
But you-

726
00:27:17.465 --> 00:27:19.925
No nonprofessional triathlete beat me out of the water,

727
00:27:19.925 --> 00:27:22.357
so that was my, like, that's the thing

728
00:27:22.357 --> 00:27:23.456
I'm gonna hang my hand on.

729
00:27:23.456 --> 00:27:24.624
But you also said you were in a group of five

730
00:27:24.624 --> 00:27:25.519
and you fell off the bat.

731
00:27:25.519 --> 00:27:26.777
I think I came out fourth.

732
00:27:26.777 --> 00:27:28.012
Yeah, you came out fourth.

733
00:27:28.012 --> 00:27:29.674
And then I looked at the results.

734
00:27:29.674 --> 00:27:32.571
Apparently, so the organization sends you the results.

735
00:27:32.571 --> 00:27:35.218
The funny thing is I get this email and they're like,

736
00:27:35.218 --> 00:27:36.259
"Here's your results.

737
00:27:36.259 --> 00:27:39.899
"We don't publish the celebrity division results."

738
00:27:39.899 --> 00:27:40.829
Oh, that was interesting.

739
00:27:40.829 --> 00:27:41.711
Why not?

740
00:27:41.711 --> 00:27:42.564
Yeah-

741
00:27:42.564 --> 00:27:44.492
'Cause like don't want that on the internet.

742
00:27:44.492 --> 00:27:47.021
Like if some TV person doesn't want anyone to know

743
00:27:47.021 --> 00:27:48.403
like how long it took them to run

744
00:27:48.403 --> 00:27:49.603
the thing or whatever.

745
00:27:49.603 --> 00:27:52.428
But anyway, so yeah, I was like, I think

746
00:27:52.428 --> 00:27:54.595
I was 13, something, 1315,

747
00:27:55.606 --> 00:27:59.156
the fastest was 1125, and then there were a couple of 12.

748
00:27:59.156 --> 00:28:02.294
So I was a minute and a half or whatever off the back

749
00:28:02.294 --> 00:28:03.990
of people who do this for a living

750
00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:05.104
who are in their twenties.

751
00:28:05.104 --> 00:28:07.058
So then I felt a lot better about myself.

752
00:28:07.058 --> 00:28:07.908
Then-

753
00:28:07.908 --> 00:28:08.759
Good job.

754
00:28:08.759 --> 00:28:11.394
Alexi gets on, or Mary gets on the bike.

755
00:28:11.394 --> 00:28:12.559
She does phenomenal.

756
00:28:12.559 --> 00:28:14.957
Like she doesn't even have a proper cycling kit.

757
00:28:14.957 --> 00:28:17.505
This is how new she is to, and she got a flat,

758
00:28:17.505 --> 00:28:19.828
like before the thing even started.

759
00:28:19.828 --> 00:28:20.688
Who fixed it?

760
00:28:20.688 --> 00:28:22.393
And she thought her break was, yeah,

761
00:28:22.393 --> 00:28:24.367
like when I'm swimming, she's like, "I have a flat."

762
00:28:24.367 --> 00:28:26.799
She got Christian to help her fix her bike.

763
00:28:26.799 --> 00:28:28.735
She got the guy who won the gold medal (laughs)

764
00:28:28.735 --> 00:28:30.375
in the Olympics Triathlon

765
00:28:30.375 --> 00:28:32.732
to help get her bike sorted out in time.

766
00:28:32.732 --> 00:28:33.668
You're in the right team.

767
00:28:33.668 --> 00:28:34.784
Yeah, that's when you know

768
00:28:34.784 --> 00:28:36.383
you're on the right team.
Yeah, which is amazing.

769
00:28:36.383 --> 00:28:37.235
(Adam laughs)

770
00:28:37.235 --> 00:28:38.699
So Mary goes off, she has a great ride,

771
00:28:38.699 --> 00:28:41.575
for somebody who has no experience.

772
00:28:41.575 --> 00:28:44.207
And Alexi, of course, is like the team captain

773
00:28:44.207 --> 00:28:46.783
and the cheerleader and she's mugging

774
00:28:46.783 --> 00:28:48.322
for the, Alexa is just.

775
00:28:48.322 --> 00:28:51.069
I wish they could both be here today to share.

776
00:28:51.069 --> 00:28:54.364
Mary had to fly back to New York.

777
00:28:54.364 --> 00:28:58.145
Maybe we'll get Alexi in here for a future "Coach's Corner,"

778
00:28:58.145 --> 00:29:00.884
we'll talk more about what went out, what went down.

779
00:29:00.884 --> 00:29:02.542
She was phenomenal and-

780
00:29:02.542 --> 00:29:03.394
Has she run?

781
00:29:03.394 --> 00:29:04.547
She do like-

782
00:29:04.547 --> 00:29:05.396
She great.

783
00:29:05.396 --> 00:29:06.907
Like sub-six minute mile.

784
00:29:06.907 --> 00:29:07.794
She's such a champ.

785
00:29:07.794 --> 00:29:08.995
I don't know what her pace was, but it was fast.

786
00:29:08.995 --> 00:29:09.852
Like, it's not a joke.

787
00:29:09.852 --> 00:29:11.053
Like she's for real.

788
00:29:11.053 --> 00:29:11.903
Yeah, yeah.

789
00:29:11.903 --> 00:29:12.753
(Rich and Adam laughing)

790
