1819
01:07:45.972 --> 01:07:49.545
of coming down the river and into the fjord,

1820
01:07:49.545 --> 01:07:54.545
and it was like you said, swimming across a motorway,

1821
01:07:54.587 --> 01:07:55.844
but filled with icebergs.

1822
01:07:55.844 --> 01:07:57.474
Is that correct?

1823
01:07:57.474 --> 01:07:58.324
Yes.

1824
01:07:58.324 --> 01:08:00.318
I want you to imagine you got the Greenland ice sheet,

1825
01:08:00.318 --> 01:08:02.998
which is the second biggest ice sheet in the world,

1826
01:08:02.998 --> 01:08:06.679
and on the edge, the ice sheet goes down valleys

1827
01:08:06.679 --> 01:08:10.158
and becomes glaciers, and at the edge of the glacier,

1828
01:08:10.158 --> 01:08:13.549
it is starting to carve and those carves into icebergs,

1829
01:08:13.549 --> 01:08:15.886
and then for a length

1830
01:08:15.886 --> 01:08:18.484
of about 60 kilometers down this long field,

1831
01:08:18.484 --> 01:08:20.403
you've got thousands and thousands

1832
01:08:20.403 --> 01:08:21.757
and thousands of icebergs.

1833
01:08:21.757 --> 01:08:23.511
And at the mouth of this field were a number

1834
01:08:23.511 --> 01:08:27.034
of really big ones, a kilometer tall, it's astonishing,

1835
01:08:27.034 --> 01:08:29.660
and they were grounded on the seabed.

1836
01:08:29.660 --> 01:08:34.110
And so I was trying to swim on the outside of these icebergs

1837
01:08:34.110 --> 01:08:35.386
across this field,

1838
01:08:35.386 --> 01:08:37.941
and then at 4:00 a.m. one morning,

1839
01:08:37.941 --> 01:08:39.531
I'll never, ever forget it,

1840
01:08:39.531 --> 01:08:41.900
I opened my curtains and I looked out and one

1841
01:08:41.900 --> 01:08:45.633
of these enormous icebergs was beginning to rotate.

1842
01:08:45.633 --> 01:08:48.773
So it was breaking up and it was rotating

1843
01:08:48.773 --> 01:08:51.765
and it opened up the flood gates

1844
01:08:51.765 --> 01:08:55.344
and literally we had 60 kilometers worth of icebergs,

1845
01:08:55.344 --> 01:08:57.238
thousands and thousands and thousands

1846
01:08:57.238 --> 01:08:59.620
going straight out to sea,

1847
01:08:59.620 --> 01:09:02.309
and within a few hours, they were 10 kilometers out to sea,

1848
01:09:02.309 --> 01:09:06.297
by the next day, they'd extended 50 kilometers to the north.

1849
01:09:06.297 --> 01:09:10.173
Wherever you looked out at sea, there were just icebergs.

1850
01:09:10.173 --> 01:09:13.553
There was no spare water anywhere, no clear water anywhere.

1851
01:09:13.553 --> 01:09:16.188
And so then having to, in a few days time,

1852
01:09:16.188 --> 01:09:19.580
there were some areas where I could do the swim, but again,

1853
01:09:19.580 --> 01:09:20.936
as soon as I got into the water,

1854
01:09:20.936 --> 01:09:23.254
it was like a motorway of ice.

1855
01:09:23.254 --> 01:09:24.237
And taking off my clothes,

1856
01:09:24.237 --> 01:09:26.152
we've got a clear routes to swim,

1857
01:09:26.152 --> 01:09:27.095
but within a short period of time,

1858
01:09:27.095 --> 01:09:29.428
now a big iceberg has moved in our way.

1859
01:09:29.428 --> 01:09:33.819
I've never seen a carving event like this in my life.

1860
01:09:33.819 --> 01:09:34.861
Mm-hmm.

1861
01:09:34.861 --> 01:09:39.272
Yeah, I think for most of us, intellectually,

1862
01:09:39.272 --> 01:09:43.288
we understand global warming and climate change

1863
01:09:43.288 --> 01:09:46.705
to be the existential threat of our time,

1864
01:09:48.020 --> 01:09:51.702
but it's purely an academic exercise for most of us.

1865
01:09:51.702 --> 01:09:54.528
We're now seeing warming events

1866
01:09:54.528 --> 01:09:56.675
and weather incidents

1867
01:09:56.675 --> 01:09:59.351
that are making it much more experiential,

1868
01:09:59.351 --> 01:10:01.775
but I think the power in your expedition

1869
01:10:01.775 --> 01:10:04.456
and what you've just experienced is it really puts

1870
01:10:04.456 --> 01:10:07.480
an exclamation point on the present reality

1871
01:10:07.480 --> 01:10:09.180
of what we're facing,

1872
01:10:09.180 --> 01:10:11.529
and my sense is that it was even worse

1873
01:10:11.529 --> 01:10:13.513
than what you expected,

1874
01:10:13.513 --> 01:10:17.146
in that you didn't realize there was gonna be this motorway

1875
01:10:17.146 --> 01:10:20.674
of smaller icebergs to have to navigate, and that in truth,

1876
01:10:20.674 --> 01:10:22.841
this scenario is far worse

1877
01:10:25.019 --> 01:10:29.671
than what we might've imagined and much more present.

1878
01:10:29.671 --> 01:10:31.506
And I think the narrative that comes out

1879
01:10:31.506 --> 01:10:35.142
of your adventure is really helping all of us

1880
01:10:35.142 --> 01:10:39.898
to embrace that reality in a more fungible, tangible way.

1881
01:10:39.898 --> 01:10:41.431
Yes, I agree with you.

1882
01:10:41.431 --> 01:10:43.545
So we've all seen the horrendous fires

1883
01:10:43.545 --> 01:10:46.223
which have been in California and in Turkey and in Greece

1884
01:10:46.223 --> 01:10:47.842
and in Siberia,

1885
01:10:47.842 --> 01:10:50.185
we seen them on television and some people

1886
01:10:50.185 --> 01:10:52.012
have witnessed them firsthand,

1887
01:10:52.012 --> 01:10:53.793
and we've all seen the dreadful floods

1888
01:10:53.793 --> 01:10:57.564
which occurred this year in Germany and in Belgium.

1889
01:10:57.564 --> 01:11:00.482
And we've heard about the melting of the ice,

1890
01:11:00.482 --> 01:11:03.243
but when you stand next to a field

1891
01:11:03.243 --> 01:11:05.565
and you watch as thousands and thousands

1892
01:11:05.565 --> 01:11:10.260
of icebergs literally are getting washed very quickly out

1893
01:11:10.260 --> 01:11:12.861
to sea, and then when you go up onto the ice sheet,

1894
01:11:12.861 --> 01:11:13.780
and everywhere you see

1895
01:11:13.780 --> 01:11:16.386
these what we call super glacier lakes,

1896
01:11:16.386 --> 01:11:19.572
so these are meltwater lakes on top of the ice sheet.

1897
01:11:19.572 --> 01:11:23.640
And what this does is that the water drills through the ice,

1898
01:11:23.640 --> 01:11:24.624
it finds cracks,

1899
01:11:24.624 --> 01:11:26.631
it goes all the way down to the bottom of the ice sheet,

1900
01:11:26.631 --> 01:11:27.545
to the bedrock,

1901
01:11:27.545 --> 01:11:31.068
making this ice sheet more and more unstable.

1902
01:11:31.068 --> 01:11:33.414
And this year, shortly before I started

1903
01:11:33.414 --> 01:11:36.254
the swim was a first time in recorded history

1904
01:11:36.254 --> 01:11:38.819
that scientists recorded rain

1905
01:11:38.819 --> 01:11:41.854
on the highest point in Greenland.

1906
01:11:41.854 --> 01:11:46.450
And so you put all these things together, all these events

1907
01:11:46.450 --> 01:11:49.458
which are happening all over the world and wildfires

1908
01:11:49.458 --> 01:11:52.534
down in Australia and the bleaching of the coral reefs,

1909
01:11:52.534 --> 01:11:55.404
you put them all together and it tells us a pattern

1910
01:11:55.404 --> 01:11:57.226
about what is happening,

1911
01:11:57.226 --> 01:12:00.338
and it shows us what will happen unless we get a grip

1912
01:12:00.338 --> 01:12:01.839
of the situation.

1913
01:12:01.839 --> 01:12:05.360
You, just one thing that people

1914
01:12:05.360 --> 01:12:09.442
might, if just for, just to make it really clear,

1915
01:12:09.442 --> 01:12:12.417
every summer, there is some melting of the ice sheet, right?

1916
01:12:12.417 --> 01:12:14.952
Just like the wildfires are not uncommon

1917
01:12:14.952 --> 01:12:18.593
or the king tide event we even saw here in Malibu,

1918
01:12:18.593 --> 01:12:20.024
which took half the beach away,

1919
01:12:20.024 --> 01:12:23.943
the beach where we are often in, where do a lot of swimming,

1920
01:12:23.943 --> 01:12:26.629
is now half the size of I've ever seen it before,

1921
01:12:26.629 --> 01:12:28.249
you were just there at Zuma.

1922
01:12:28.249 --> 01:12:33.249
And how do you, like for people who aren't as familiar,

1923
01:12:33.315 --> 01:12:35.904
how do you parse what's normal

1924
01:12:35.904 --> 01:12:38.600
and what's become augmented or extreme?

1925
01:12:38.600 --> 01:12:41.090
Or is that just not something that's even worth discussing?

1926
01:12:41.090 --> 01:12:42.788
Now, when you talk about the rain on Greenland,

1927
01:12:42.788 --> 01:12:43.991
that's one thing, it's never happened before,

1928
01:12:43.991 --> 01:12:46.923
but how do you explain to people what's happening

1929
01:12:46.923 --> 01:12:50.114
is just a bigger version of the natural cycle

1930
01:12:50.114 --> 01:12:52.445
and therefore it's dangerous?

1931
01:12:52.445 --> 01:12:54.056
Yes, so you're absolutely right.

1932
01:12:54.056 --> 01:12:56.488
From time immemorial,

1933
01:12:56.488 --> 01:13:00.272
glaciers have carved icebergs and icebergs have gone out

1934
01:13:00.272 --> 01:13:04.985
to sea, but it's the speed and the scale of the crisis,

1935
01:13:04.985 --> 01:13:06.025
of the climate crisis

1936
01:13:06.025 --> 01:13:08.422
which is taking scientists by surprise.

1937
01:13:08.422 --> 01:13:09.896
And just to give you a practical example,

1938
01:13:09.896 --> 01:13:13.217
so we've been talking about ice, which is on land.

1939
01:13:13.217 --> 01:13:14.758
Let me give you another example about ice

1940
01:13:14.758 --> 01:13:17.080
which is in the sea, so sea ice.

1941
01:13:17.080 --> 01:13:21.719
Back in 2005, I did a swim in the Norwegian part

1942
01:13:21.719 --> 01:13:25.068
of the Arctic, north of an island called Spitsbergen,

1943
01:13:25.068 --> 01:13:29.677
and there in summer, the water was three degrees centigrade.

1944
01:13:29.677 --> 01:13:32.269
I went back there 12 years later,

1945
01:13:32.269 --> 01:13:34.638
the water wasn't three degrees centigrade,

1946
01:13:34.638 --> 01:13:37.204
now it was 10 degrees centigrade.

1947
01:13:37.204 --> 01:13:38.437
Mm-hmm.

1948
01:13:38.437 --> 01:13:42.712
So going from three, 10 in just 12 years,

1949
01:13:42.712 --> 01:13:45.124
and the scientists are telling me that is not normal.

1950
01:13:45.124 --> 01:13:45.974
Mm-hmm.

1951
01:13:45.974 --> 01:13:50.327
That is an impact, simply warming the planet.

1952
01:13:50.327 --> 01:13:53.113
Luis, you mentioned that you've been doing these swims

1953
01:13:53.113 --> 01:13:54.909
for over three decades at this point.

1954
01:13:54.909 --> 01:13:57.774
I'm curious about the intersection

1955
01:13:57.774 --> 01:14:00.975
of your interest in environmentalism

1956
01:14:00.975 --> 01:14:02.719
with with your swimming,

1957
01:14:02.719 --> 01:14:05.243
like which came first?

1958
01:14:05.243 --> 01:14:07.964
And how do they inform each other?

1959
01:14:07.964 --> 01:14:09.423
Swimming came first.

1960
01:14:09.423 --> 01:14:10.465
Swimming came first.

1961
01:14:10.465 --> 01:14:14.256
So (laughs) I had my purpose, first proper swimming lesson

1962
01:14:14.256 --> 01:14:17.231
very late in life when I was 17.

1963
01:14:17.231 --> 01:14:19.287
I grew up in, initially in Britain,

1964
01:14:19.287 --> 01:14:22.363
but then I moved out to Cape town.

1965
01:14:22.363 --> 01:14:24.458
And for those of your listeners who've been to Cape town,

1966
01:14:24.458 --> 01:14:26.414
it's just an incredible place 'cause on the one side,

1967
01:14:26.414 --> 01:14:28.230
you've got the Indian Ocean, on the other side

1968
01:14:28.230 --> 01:14:29.771
you've got the Atlantic Ocean,

1969
01:14:29.771 --> 01:14:33.561
and my high school history class looked out

1970
01:14:33.561 --> 01:14:35.013
over the Atlantic ocean,

1971
01:14:35.013 --> 01:14:38.049
and in the distance, if I put my head out the window,

1972
01:14:38.049 --> 01:14:39.857
I could see Robin Island,

1973
01:14:39.857 --> 01:14:43.134
and I don't even know where the idea came from,

1974
01:14:43.134 --> 01:14:45.912
but I just wanted to swim to this island.

1975
01:14:45.912 --> 01:14:47.024
This was 1987.

1976
01:14:47.024 --> 01:14:48.888
This was a time when Nelson Mandela

1977
01:14:48.888 --> 01:14:52.818
and many people were imprisoned in South Africa.

1978
01:14:52.818 --> 01:14:55.272
Anyway, I got permission to do this swim,

1979
01:14:55.272 --> 01:14:57.022
and I barely made it.

1980
01:14:59.744 --> 01:15:03.520
And, but afterwards, every single year,

1981
01:15:03.520 --> 01:15:05.084
I've just tried to do another swim.

1982
01:15:05.084 --> 01:15:05.963
It really grabbed me.

1983
01:15:05.963 --> 01:15:08.288
I loved swimming so much.

1984
01:15:08.288 --> 01:15:12.324
And so every year I've done a bigger, harder, tougher

1985
01:15:12.324 --> 01:15:15.245
and inevitably colder swim.

1986
01:15:15.245 --> 01:15:17.749
And then there was a time in the early 2000s

1987
01:15:17.749 --> 01:15:22.268
where I began to notice how the oceans were really changing.

1988
01:15:22.268 --> 01:15:25.099
So whether it be the, what was happening in the Arctic

1989
01:15:25.099 --> 01:15:27.970
with the sea ice, we're just seeing less fish in the world,

1990
01:15:27.970 --> 01:15:30.711
with plastic all over our beaches,

1991
01:15:30.711 --> 01:15:33.542
and then there came a time when I realized I had to stand up

1992
01:15:33.542 --> 01:15:36.887
and talk about what I was seeing in the water oceans.

1993
01:15:36.887 --> 01:15:37.740
Mm-hmm.

1994
01:15:37.740 --> 01:15:42.035
And so tying the power of your swimming capabilities

1995
01:15:42.035 --> 01:15:46.952
to activism has become like your advocacy and your passion.

1996
01:15:48.895 --> 01:15:52.525
What is your sense of impact with all of this?

1997
01:15:52.525 --> 01:15:55.113
Like, are you encouraged by the results

1998
01:15:55.113 --> 01:15:57.437
of getting out there and doing things

1999
01:15:57.437 --> 01:16:00.354
that draw a lot of attention and interest

2000
01:16:00.354 --> 01:16:01.602
in what you're doing?

2001
01:16:01.602 --> 01:16:04.231
Like, how do we translate the inspiration

2002
01:16:04.231 --> 01:16:07.564
that we can garner from your epics swims

2003
01:16:09.108 --> 01:16:10.525
into real change?

2004
01:16:12.938 --> 01:16:14.975
It's really hard to measure the impact.

2005
01:16:14.975 --> 01:16:18.542
Somebody may look at my sermon and hopefully be inspired

2006
01:16:18.542 --> 01:16:22.036
to protect the environment in whichever area

2007
01:16:22.036 --> 01:16:23.786
that they operate in.

2008
01:16:24.732 --> 01:16:27.713
So it's very, very hard to measure it.

2009
01:16:27.713 --> 01:16:29.646
It's not like, let's say, selling shoes.

2010
01:16:29.646 --> 01:16:32.437
If you sell shoes, you know how many you sold at the end

2011
01:16:32.437 --> 01:16:33.894
of each month and the end of each year

2012
01:16:33.894 --> 01:16:36.221
and you know when you're being successful.

2013
01:16:36.221 --> 01:16:38.030
What I've concentrated on the last couple of years

2014
01:16:38.030 --> 01:16:41.003
is creating what we call Marine protected areas,

2015
01:16:41.003 --> 01:16:43.581
and Marine protected areas are like national parks,

2016
01:16:43.581 --> 01:16:45.120
but they're in the sea.

2017
01:16:45.120 --> 01:16:48.475
They prevents overfishing in these areas,

2018
01:16:48.475 --> 01:16:52.558
and the big success came a few years ago, 2016,

2019
01:16:52.558 --> 01:16:55.415
when I worked with John Kerry and a number of other people

2020
01:16:55.415 --> 01:16:58.600
to help create a very, very big Marine protected area

2021
01:16:58.600 --> 01:17:00.636
down in Antarctica.

2022
01:17:00.636 --> 01:17:03.532
So that Marine protected area is the size of Britain,

2023
01:17:03.532 --> 01:17:06.019
France, Germany, Italy, all put together.

2024
01:17:06.019 --> 01:17:08.814
It's the biggest protected area in the world,

2025
01:17:08.814 --> 01:17:11.261
but, and would've thought I'd be really happy about this,

2026
01:17:11.261 --> 01:17:12.470
but I'm not.

2027
01:17:12.470 --> 01:17:14.440
We're now in a race against time.

2028
01:17:14.440 --> 01:17:15.780
We're trying to create

2029
01:17:15.780 --> 01:17:19.193
another three big Marine protected areas around Antarctica,

2030
01:17:19.193 --> 01:17:22.819
and we're also working to try and get 30%

2031
01:17:22.819 --> 01:17:27.213
of the world's oceans now protected by 2030.

2032
01:17:27.213 --> 01:17:28.986
I think really interesting,

2033
01:17:28.986 --> 01:17:30.501
some of the stuff that you've done,

2034
01:17:30.501 --> 01:17:32.926
including like, I guess the last one before this,

2035
01:17:32.926 --> 01:17:35.025
you were actually swimming in a melting glacier.

2036
01:17:35.025 --> 01:17:39.743
Like you found a riverine glacial melt in Antarctica,

2037
01:17:39.743 --> 01:17:43.947
and you actually got inside a glacier and swam, I guess,

2038
01:17:43.947 --> 01:17:47.283
a kilometer inside this glacial river.

2039
01:17:47.283 --> 01:17:50.839
And that was an incredible moment as well.

2040
01:17:50.839 --> 01:17:55.694
And so you've found these really interesting places to go,

2041
01:17:55.694 --> 01:17:58.425
but something that people might not know of

2042
01:17:58.425 --> 01:18:01.690
is your partnership with Slava Fetisov,

2043
01:18:01.690 --> 01:18:03.857
the hockey star in Russia,

2044
01:18:04.701 --> 01:18:06.002
and I wanted to bring that up

2045
01:18:06.002 --> 01:18:10.618
just because, especially in America, we have this crazy kind

2046
01:18:10.618 --> 01:18:15.285
of black and white Russia versus the United States thing

2047
01:18:16.505 --> 01:18:18.426
that's been going on my entire life,

2048
01:18:18.426 --> 01:18:20.377
but really there's some really great environmentalists

2049
01:18:20.377 --> 01:18:21.358
on both sides.

2050
01:18:21.358 --> 01:18:23.084
Can you explain your partnership with him

2051
01:18:23.084 --> 01:18:25.557
and how he was involved in Antarctica, and just in general,

2052
01:18:25.557 --> 01:18:28.331
how he's an ally in climate?

2053
01:18:28.331 --> 01:18:29.180
Yeah.

2054
01:18:29.180 --> 01:18:30.669
So Slava is a very special person,

2055
01:18:30.669 --> 01:18:32.586
and for your listeners who don't know him,

2056
01:18:32.586 --> 01:18:33.434
he used to be the Captain

2057
01:18:33.434 --> 01:18:35.772
of the former Soviet Ice Hockey Team,

2058
01:18:35.772 --> 01:18:39.278
and then he was the first Russian to come over

2059
01:18:39.278 --> 01:18:41.637
and play professional ice hockey

2060
01:18:41.637 --> 01:18:44.040
in the United States of America.

2061
01:18:44.040 --> 01:18:46.783
The way I got to meet him was I was trying

2062
01:18:46.783 --> 01:18:49.109
to get this area done in Antarctica protected

2063
01:18:49.109 --> 01:18:51.893
called the Ross Sea, and under international law,

2064
01:18:51.893 --> 01:18:55.264
25 countries had to agree at, and one of those was Russia,

2065
01:18:55.264 --> 01:18:57.633
another of those was China,

2066
01:18:57.633 --> 01:19:01.062
and all the countries agreed except Russia and China.

2067
01:19:01.062 --> 01:19:04.064
And no matter how many times, it took 17 years,

2068
01:19:04.064 --> 01:19:05.999
diplomats were going backwards and forwards trying

2069
01:19:05.999 --> 01:19:07.972
to get these last two countries to agree it,

2070
01:19:07.972 --> 01:19:11.402
and they weren't able to agree it.

2071
01:19:11.402 --> 01:19:13.510
It was President Obama's last term,

2072
01:19:13.510 --> 01:19:14.900
and Obama was very passionate

2073
01:19:14.900 --> 01:19:16.941
about creating Marine protected areas,

2074
01:19:16.941 --> 01:19:18.895
he sent John Kerry through to Beijing,

2075
01:19:18.895 --> 01:19:21.805
and John was able to get the Chinese to agree it.

2076
01:19:21.805 --> 01:19:23.773
For the last hurdle, was obviously the Russians,

2077
01:19:23.773 --> 01:19:26.912
and I just, it sounds crazy,

2078
01:19:26.912 --> 01:19:31.095
I had this belief that if I could just go down there and do

2079
01:19:31.095 --> 01:19:33.550
a swim in this place and show the beauty of it and show

2080
01:19:33.550 --> 01:19:37.405
how magnificent it is and film the humpback whales and film

2081
01:19:37.405 --> 01:19:39.480
the emperor penguins

2082
01:19:39.480 --> 01:19:44.111
and all these other magnificent animals, this is their home,

2083
01:19:44.111 --> 01:19:46.605
we need to protect it.

2084
01:19:46.605 --> 01:19:50.043
This place was on the edge of being seriously overfished.

2085
01:19:50.043 --> 01:19:52.620
If I could do that and I could go to Russia and meet

2086
01:19:52.620 --> 01:19:54.465
with Russia's leadership,

2087
01:19:54.465 --> 01:19:57.010
I hope that I could persuade them that we must protect

2088
01:19:57.010 --> 01:19:58.718
this last great wilderness,

2089
01:19:58.718 --> 01:20:01.547
but I'm half British and half south African,

2090
01:20:01.547 --> 01:20:02.849
I had no friends in Russia,

2091
01:20:02.849 --> 01:20:06.516
but somebody introduced me to Slava Fetisov.

2092
01:20:07.452 --> 01:20:08.576
And I'll never forget what he said.

2093
01:20:08.576 --> 01:20:09.765
He said, "You know, Lewis,

2094
01:20:09.765 --> 01:20:12.057
"I'm a defense man,

2095
01:20:12.057 --> 01:20:15.930
"and the world needs more defenders and protectors.

2096
01:20:15.930 --> 01:20:19.339
"Do your swim, come here and I'll introduce you

2097
01:20:19.339 --> 01:20:21.018
"to who you need to meet."

2098
01:20:21.018 --> 01:20:23.332
So I went down there, I did the swim.

2099
01:20:23.332 --> 01:20:25.236
I then immediately got on a plane and I flew

2100
01:20:25.236 --> 01:20:28.433
from Cape town through to Moscow,

2101
01:20:28.433 --> 01:20:29.897
just not knowing what to expect,

2102
01:20:29.897 --> 01:20:32.798
and I arrived there, and there was Slava.

2103
01:20:32.798 --> 01:20:37.239
Slava Fetisov in Russia is the biggest sporting name, still,

2104
01:20:37.239 --> 01:20:39.072
he just turned 60, 61,

2105
01:20:40.457 --> 01:20:43.221
he's still a huge name there, very, very influential,

2106
01:20:43.221 --> 01:20:44.495
and he's a senator,

2107
01:20:44.495 --> 01:20:47.887
and he took me literally from minister to minister,

2108
01:20:47.887 --> 01:20:50.419
to minister, to the security minister,

2109
01:20:50.419 --> 01:20:54.022
to the closest advisors to President Putin,

2110
01:20:54.022 --> 01:20:55.344
and over a period of two years,

2111
01:20:55.344 --> 01:20:57.720
I shuttled backwards and forwards to Washington.

2112
01:20:57.720 --> 01:21:01.373
And then that day when Russia signed the deal,

2113
01:21:01.373 --> 01:21:05.347
so the last country of the 25 countries to agree it,

2114
01:21:05.347 --> 01:21:08.383
it was happiest day of both of our lives,

2115
01:21:08.383 --> 01:21:12.412
'cause we realized that we need to talk to each other,

2116
01:21:12.412 --> 01:21:14.095
we need to work together.

2117
01:21:14.095 --> 01:21:17.013
These global problems cannot be solved just by America,

2118
01:21:17.013 --> 01:21:18.373
or just by Britain.

2119
01:21:18.373 --> 01:21:22.565
Russia is a country and it operates in 11 times zones,

2120
01:21:22.565 --> 01:21:25.048
it go from Vladivostok all the way to Saint Petersburg,

2121
01:21:25.048 --> 01:21:26.558
11 time zones.

2122
01:21:26.558 --> 01:21:28.344
It owns half the Arctic.

2123
01:21:28.344 --> 01:21:30.530
It's got huge natural resources.

2124
01:21:30.530 --> 01:21:32.901
You think of China, what a massive country China is?

2125
01:21:32.901 --> 01:21:33.751
India.

2126
01:21:33.751 --> 01:21:36.176
We cannot solve these big global problems,

2127
01:21:36.176 --> 01:21:39.275
whether it be climate change, overfishing, pollution,

2128
01:21:39.275 --> 01:21:43.002
any of these without all these countries working together.

2129
01:21:43.002 --> 01:21:44.847
And that's why these environmental diplomats

2130
01:21:44.847 --> 01:21:49.615
are so important because they're able to work together

2131
01:21:49.615 --> 01:21:52.439
and listen to the dialogue of the other side.

2132
01:21:52.439 --> 01:21:53.288
Yeah.

2133
01:21:53.288 --> 01:21:56.261
And with that, we're on the cusp of COP26,

2134
01:21:56.261 --> 01:22:00.167
and in Glasgow, I think you're attending that.

2135
01:22:00.167 --> 01:22:04.584
Like, what is your hope for what may transpire there?

2136
01:22:05.751 --> 01:22:06.911
Yeah.

2137
01:22:06.911 --> 01:22:11.078
Well, my hope for COP26 is that finally we realize

2138
01:22:14.711 --> 01:22:18.185
that we're in the last chance, that we really are.

2139
01:22:18.185 --> 01:22:21.297
So what I saw in Greenland in terms of the melting

2140
01:22:21.297 --> 01:22:25.526
was so worrying, very, very, very worrying.

2141
01:22:25.526 --> 01:22:28.327
And I want bring my message by what I've seen there

2142
01:22:28.327 --> 01:22:29.808
and bring it to Glasgow.

2143
01:22:29.808 --> 01:22:32.718
Ice is essential for life on earth,

2144
01:22:32.718 --> 01:22:34.262
'cause what I started at keeps our planet

2145
01:22:34.262 --> 01:22:37.269
within a temperature range in which we can live.

2146
01:22:37.269 --> 01:22:38.945
And I always say to politicians, no ice, no life.

2147
01:22:38.945 --> 01:22:42.479
If we allow the polar regions to melt and we allow

2148
01:22:42.479 --> 01:22:45.141
the glaciers in the Himalayas to melt,

2149
01:22:45.141 --> 01:22:46.835
we will not be able to survive.

2150
01:22:46.835 --> 01:22:49.498
Ice is as important as the air we breathe.

2151
01:22:49.498 --> 01:22:53.322
And I'll go there with a message, please,

2152
01:22:53.322 --> 01:22:54.964
we cannot have these commit.

2153
01:22:54.964 --> 01:22:59.964
I cringe whenever I hear a world leader make a commitment

2154
01:23:00.290 --> 01:23:03.768
for 2050 and 2060, which a lot of them are making now.

2155
01:23:03.768 --> 01:23:06.227
There's no political leader or business in the world

2156
01:23:06.227 --> 01:23:09.871
that won't make a commitment for 2050, 2060,

2157
01:23:09.871 --> 01:23:11.692
and we know perfectly well they won't be around there

2158
01:23:11.692 --> 01:23:13.041
to deliver on it.

2159
01:23:13.041 --> 01:23:15.363
We need shorter commitments,

2160
01:23:15.363 --> 01:23:18.685
but not only the commitments, we now need action.

2161
01:23:18.685 --> 01:23:21.179
The time for talking, talking is important,

2162
01:23:21.179 --> 01:23:22.497
getting consensus is important,

2163
01:23:22.497 --> 01:23:24.414
but the time for action is right now.

2164
01:23:24.414 --> 01:23:28.194
Yeah, it seems that the gravamen of the conversation

2165
01:23:28.194 --> 01:23:30.988
is around slowing this process down.

2166
01:23:30.988 --> 01:23:33.718
We had Paul Hawken on the show the other week,

2167
01:23:33.718 --> 01:23:36.096
and he said (laughs) he said, "Nobody gets up

2168
01:23:36.096 --> 01:23:38.664
"in the morning excited about mitigation, right?

2169
01:23:38.664 --> 01:23:41.947
"We need to be talking about draw-down, like reversing this.

2170
01:23:41.947 --> 01:23:46.270
"We need to invest in these regenerative solutions."

2171
01:23:46.270 --> 01:23:49.344
And yet from a political perspective,

2172
01:23:49.344 --> 01:23:50.978
that becomes problematic,

2173
01:23:50.978 --> 01:23:52.928
and the safest route is always to talk

2174
01:23:52.928 --> 01:23:55.255
about this mitigation idea

2175
01:23:55.255 --> 01:23:57.183
or how we can reduce these emissions

2176
01:23:57.183 --> 01:24:00.769
as opposed to really, in my opinion,

2177
01:24:00.769 --> 01:24:04.903
overinvest in the solutions that will be most impactful

2178
01:24:04.903 --> 01:24:09.511
in terms of draw-down and this idea of regeneration.

2179
01:24:09.511 --> 01:24:13.244
Well, the interesting thing is if we miss the time now,

2180
01:24:13.244 --> 01:24:17.499
so if we allow, just to put this in perspective,

2181
01:24:17.499 --> 01:24:20.832
we're going into what's called UN COP26.

2182
01:24:21.725 --> 01:24:25.316
So COP26 means there've been 26 of these conferences.

2183
01:24:25.316 --> 01:24:28.121
They've lost it 27 years.

2184
01:24:28.121 --> 01:24:28.981
Okay?

2185
01:24:28.981 --> 01:24:33.354
So a large portion of the world's population were not alive

2186
01:24:33.354 --> 01:24:37.480
when we started these negotiations to try and put a halt

2187
01:24:37.480 --> 01:24:39.364
to the climate crisis.

2188
01:24:39.364 --> 01:24:40.214
Okay?

2189
01:24:40.214 --> 01:24:42.651
And if we miss this opportunity,

2190
01:24:42.651 --> 01:24:44.649
and if it's just a talking shop,

2191
01:24:44.649 --> 01:24:47.000
then no amount of political goodwill,

2192
01:24:47.000 --> 01:24:49.071
no amount of technology which you talk about,

2193
01:24:49.071 --> 01:24:52.135
no amount of investment is gonna be able

2194
01:24:52.135 --> 01:24:54.859
to solve this crisis because you pass as a scientist,

2195
01:24:54.859 --> 01:24:57.196
say certain tipping points,

2196
01:24:57.196 --> 01:24:59.023
and there are a number of scientists who believe

2197
01:24:59.023 --> 01:25:02.739
we have already passed some tipping points in terms

2198
01:25:02.739 --> 01:25:03.957
of the Greenland ice sheet.

2199
01:25:03.957 --> 01:25:05.808
If the whole Greenland ice sheet were melt,

2200
01:25:05.808 --> 01:25:10.717
that's a seven-meter sea level rise that impacts everybody.

2201
01:25:10.717 --> 01:25:14.794
Yeah, we've seen the floods, like you said,

2202
01:25:14.794 --> 01:25:16.255
in Europe, we saw floods in New York City,

2203
01:25:16.255 --> 01:25:19.304
it wasn't even a hurricane and it caused

2204
01:25:19.304 --> 01:25:22.217
the catastrophic floods, killed dozens of people.

2205
01:25:22.217 --> 01:25:25.467
We've seen these climate events happen.

2206
01:25:28.535 --> 01:25:30.930
In Greenland, originally you were thinking

2207
01:25:30.930 --> 01:25:34.641
of a bigger swim of swimming the entire length

2208
01:25:34.641 --> 01:25:37.011
of what has receded,

2209
01:25:37.011 --> 01:25:38.948
a part of the ice sheet that's already melted.

2210
01:25:38.948 --> 01:25:39.821
Correct?

2211
01:25:39.821 --> 01:25:41.528
What was that number again,

2212
01:25:41.528 --> 01:25:46.122
like since from the beginning of the COPS from 27 years ago?

2213
01:25:46.122 --> 01:25:46.972
Yeah.

2214
01:25:46.972 --> 01:25:48.639
So initially in a...

2215
01:25:49.831 --> 01:25:52.927
I've always got an Atlas opening in my office,

2216
01:25:52.927 --> 01:25:55.601
and I'm always looking at and thinking to myself,

2217
01:25:55.601 --> 01:25:57.196
where can I do a swim here

2218
01:25:57.196 --> 01:25:59.587
that's gonna highlight an issue, be it pollution,

2219
01:25:59.587 --> 01:26:01.941
be it climate change, be it overfishing?

2220
01:26:01.941 --> 01:26:03.164
Where can I do a swim?

2221
01:26:03.164 --> 01:26:06.164
And creativity is not an aha moment.

2222
01:26:08.463 --> 01:26:10.021
It's an, a duh, moment.

2223
01:26:10.021 --> 01:26:13.531
It means, uh, why did I not see this great swim?

2224
01:26:13.531 --> 01:26:16.812
And so there was a moment when I thought, wow!

2225
01:26:16.812 --> 01:26:21.578
Why don't I go to Greenland and do a swim in Ilulissat

2226
01:26:21.578 --> 01:26:23.452
and start where the glacier

2227
01:26:23.452 --> 01:26:28.369
was 27 years ago and swim all the way to where it is today?

2228
01:26:31.770 --> 01:26:35.435
That was a bit naive of me because there's so much ice

2229
01:26:35.435 --> 01:26:38.425
pouring down, it's literally like a wall of ice,

2230
01:26:38.425 --> 01:26:40.380
so much ice now coming down

2231
01:26:40.380 --> 01:26:43.014
that that swim wouldn't have been possible.

2232
01:26:43.014 --> 01:26:44.251
So as soon as I got there,

2233
01:26:44.251 --> 01:26:45.989
I realized that I had to swim across it

2234
01:26:45.989 --> 01:26:48.169
rather than do something like that,

2235
01:26:48.169 --> 01:26:49.552
which would have been, frankly,

2236
01:26:49.552 --> 01:26:53.084
would have been a lot easier to tell as a media story,

2237
01:26:53.084 --> 01:26:57.442
this is where the glacier was 27 years ago,

2238
01:26:57.442 --> 01:27:00.917
and there it is all the way up there now.

2239
01:27:00.917 --> 01:27:02.592
Yeah, it's several kilometers, right?

2240
01:27:02.592 --> 01:27:04.188
Or several miles?

2241
01:27:04.188 --> 01:27:06.771
Follows, since 100 years ago,

2242
01:27:07.624 --> 01:27:09.157
it's gone over 60 kilometers.

2243
01:27:09.157 --> 01:27:10.005
Wow!

2244
01:27:10.005 --> 01:27:12.588
So it's receded an awful lot.

2245
01:27:13.925 --> 01:27:15.741
And as I said, at the moment now, it's receding,

2246
01:27:15.741 --> 01:27:20.491
so it's moving at a speed of 14 meters per day in summer.

2247
01:27:22.142 --> 01:27:23.042
It's crazy.

2248
01:27:23.042 --> 01:27:24.226
It's truly astonishing.

2249
01:27:24.226 --> 01:27:27.893
The other thing that you notice when you're up right next

2250
01:27:27.893 --> 01:27:29.784
to the glacier as it's carving

2251
01:27:29.784 --> 01:27:31.248
is just how much water there is.

2252
01:27:31.248 --> 01:27:33.591
There are waterfalls coming on the side,

2253
01:27:33.591 --> 01:27:35.134
you've got water coming from the side,

2254
01:27:35.134 --> 01:27:37.460
you've got water coming underneath as I talked

2255
01:27:37.460 --> 01:27:39.052
about those lakes on the top,

2256
01:27:39.052 --> 01:27:41.644
you've got water draining through and coming out.

2257
01:27:41.644 --> 01:27:45.572
It is a very, very dynamic situation there right now.

2258
01:27:45.572 --> 01:27:48.778
And remember, this is just one glacier

2259
01:27:48.778 --> 01:27:51.587
on the west coast of Greenland,

2260
01:27:51.587 --> 01:27:55.019
but this is happening in many glaciers around the world,

2261
01:27:55.019 --> 01:27:56.776
in the Arctic, in the Antarctic,

2262
01:27:56.776 --> 01:27:59.554
and obviously in the mountainous regions.

2263
01:27:59.554 --> 01:28:02.765
And the mountainous regions are another scenario altogether.

2264
01:28:02.765 --> 01:28:05.672
And the reason for that is because those glaces provide

2265
01:28:05.672 --> 01:28:06.951
a constant water supply.

2266
01:28:06.951 --> 01:28:07.801
Mm-hmm.

2267
01:28:07.801 --> 01:28:09.578
So you think about the glaces in the Himalayas,

2268
01:28:09.578 --> 01:28:12.233
they provide water to India, to China, to Bangladesh,

2269
01:28:12.233 --> 01:28:14.242
to Burma, to Pakistan.

2270
01:28:14.242 --> 01:28:16.186
People need water.

2271
01:28:16.186 --> 01:28:18.553
Water is essential for life on earth.

2272
01:28:18.553 --> 01:28:22.345
And as those glaciers retreat, as a melt away,

2273
01:28:22.345 --> 01:28:24.217
there goes at water supply.

2274
01:28:24.217 --> 01:28:26.897
So those areas then become a cocktail for conflict.

2275
01:28:26.897 --> 01:28:29.442
People need to move to water.

2276
01:28:29.442 --> 01:28:31.765
You had mentioned that the Greenland swim

2277
01:28:31.765 --> 01:28:36.182
was among, if not the hardest swim that you had done.

2278
01:28:37.470 --> 01:28:41.112
Walk us through the experience of getting in that water.

2279
01:28:41.112 --> 01:28:44.007
I guess it was around zero celsius, correct?

2280
01:28:44.007 --> 01:28:47.220
And how you kind of trained and prepared yourself

2281
01:28:47.220 --> 01:28:50.374
to endure that and what it actually felt like

2282
01:28:50.374 --> 01:28:55.241
when you were (laughs) in that frigid cold.

2283
01:28:55.241 --> 01:28:57.605
Rich, the water is so cold,

2284
01:28:57.605 --> 01:28:58.458
(Adam and Rich laughing)

2285
01:28:58.458 --> 01:29:02.050
so it ranged between (laughs) it ranged

2286
01:29:02.050 --> 01:29:04.816
between zero and three degrees.

2287
01:29:04.816 --> 01:29:08.483
And swimming in cold water is the only sport

2288
01:29:10.705 --> 01:29:12.134
in the whole world

2289
01:29:12.134 --> 01:29:15.272
that the more experience you have, the harder it becomes.

2290
01:29:15.272 --> 01:29:16.169
(Adam and Rich laughing)

2291
01:29:16.169 --> 01:29:19.085
And the reason for that is, so let's just say,

2292
01:29:19.085 --> 01:29:20.189
you're playing tennis.

2293
01:29:20.189 --> 01:29:22.499
The more balls you hit, the easier it becomes,

2294
01:29:22.499 --> 01:29:23.626
what it should.

2295
01:29:23.626 --> 01:29:24.464
Okay.

2296
01:29:24.464 --> 01:29:26.550
With cold water swimming,

2297
01:29:26.550 --> 01:29:27.810
the more you do, the harder it becomes,

2298
01:29:27.810 --> 01:29:30.344
and that is because when you've been really, really,

2299
01:29:30.344 --> 01:29:33.438
really cold, you never forget it.

2300
01:29:33.438 --> 01:29:34.639
It's deep down in your bones.

2301
01:29:34.639 --> 01:29:37.464
And so every single subsequent you swim you're gonna do,

2302
01:29:37.464 --> 01:29:39.460
you have to forget about what happened there.

2303
01:29:39.460 --> 01:29:41.811
I had to forget about that swim in 2007

2304
01:29:41.811 --> 01:29:43.595
when I swim across the north pole.

2305
01:29:43.595 --> 01:29:44.747
I had to forget the panic

2306
01:29:44.747 --> 01:29:46.843
when I was swimming down in the Ross Sea

2307
01:29:46.843 --> 01:29:48.340
while gasping for air

2308
01:29:48.340 --> 01:29:49.594
in a glacier lake on Mount Everest,

2309
01:29:49.594 --> 01:29:51.571
you got to forget it and you've gotta be ready

2310
01:29:51.571 --> 01:29:52.486
for the new swim.

2311
01:29:52.486 --> 01:29:54.933
So the training package was a long package.

2312
01:29:54.933 --> 01:29:57.446
I then did the Acclimatization Iceland,

2313
01:29:57.446 --> 01:30:00.068
and then you arrive in Greenland,

2314
01:30:00.068 --> 01:30:01.604
and now I'm gonna get into the water.

2315
01:30:01.604 --> 01:30:05.264
And as I slowly lower myself down that ladder (chuckles)

2316
01:30:05.264 --> 01:30:07.431
it's just incredibly cold.

2317
01:30:08.331 --> 01:30:13.258
And early in the morning, I've taken a temperature capsule,

2318
01:30:13.258 --> 01:30:16.988
and what that does is it tells my doctor on the boat

2319
01:30:16.988 --> 01:30:20.695
exactly what's happening inside me realtime.

2320
01:30:20.695 --> 01:30:23.831
And the astonishing thing happens when I lower myself

2321
01:30:23.831 --> 01:30:24.807
into really cold water,

2322
01:30:24.807 --> 01:30:27.300
actually my core body temperature rises.

2323
01:30:27.300 --> 01:30:29.776
So it rises from normal core body temperature,

2324
01:30:29.776 --> 01:30:32.176
which is about 37, up to 38,

2325
01:30:32.176 --> 01:30:35.544
and then I normally swim for about 10 minutes,

2326
01:30:35.544 --> 01:30:37.353
and during that time,

2327
01:30:37.353 --> 01:30:40.008
my core body temperature maintain the same level,

2328
01:30:40.008 --> 01:30:42.506
and on some occasions even increased.

2329
01:30:42.506 --> 01:30:45.200
But then when I get out and the cold blood in my arms

2330
01:30:45.200 --> 01:30:47.532
and legs returns to my core,

2331
01:30:47.532 --> 01:30:49.895
then my core body temperature drops.

2332
01:30:49.895 --> 01:30:52.864
And so then it's quickly into a sleeping bag

2333
01:30:52.864 --> 01:30:55.171
and then a hot water, three hot water bottles,

2334
01:30:55.171 --> 01:30:57.235
hot chocolates, and it would take me

2335
01:30:57.235 --> 01:31:00.205
between two and two and a half hours to rewarm

2336
01:31:00.205 --> 01:31:03.523
after each of those swims, and I was doing it twice a day.

2337
01:31:03.523 --> 01:31:06.555
So after I'd finished my first swim, I'd recovered,

2338
01:31:06.555 --> 01:31:09.064
I'd eaten a bit of lunch, then back in again

2339
01:31:09.064 --> 01:31:10.080
for the second session.

2340
01:31:10.080 --> 01:31:13.038
It was unrelenting and it was-

2341
01:31:13.038 --> 01:31:14.459
So 10-minutes swims?

2342
01:31:14.459 --> 01:31:18.361
It takes two hours-plus to warm up from a 10-minute swim.

2343
01:31:18.361 --> 01:31:19.307
Correct.

2344
01:31:19.307 --> 01:31:20.592
And you're in.

2345
01:31:20.592 --> 01:31:25.198
And in the old days, I just used to dive in.

2346
01:31:25.198 --> 01:31:26.629
I used to dive in,

2347
01:31:26.629 --> 01:31:29.089
I used to swim as fast and as quick as I could, get out,

2348
01:31:29.089 --> 01:31:30.788
get into a hot shower.

2349
01:31:30.788 --> 01:31:33.322
We tried that in some of the training,

2350
01:31:33.322 --> 01:31:35.955
that is a worst possible thing you could do.

2351
01:31:35.955 --> 01:31:38.958
The science now is quite clear you need to lower yourself,

2352
01:31:38.958 --> 01:31:40.908
you need to be able to control your breathing,

2353
01:31:40.908 --> 01:31:42.683
not have what they call the cold-shock response,

2354
01:31:42.683 --> 01:31:45.850
and then once you're ready, get going,

2355
01:31:46.721 --> 01:31:50.057
and then swim as fast and as hard as you can.

2356
01:31:50.057 --> 01:31:51.883
Swimming's also the only sport in the world

2357
01:31:51.883 --> 01:31:53.977
which operates on three axes.

2358
01:31:53.977 --> 01:31:55.802
So your head moves left or right,

2359
01:31:55.802 --> 01:31:57.291
your arms move around and around,

2360
01:31:57.291 --> 01:31:58.692
your legs move up and down.

2361
01:31:58.692 --> 01:32:00.687
And if any one of those is not in sync,

2362
01:32:00.687 --> 01:32:02.650
you're gonna be fighting the sea.

2363
01:32:02.650 --> 01:32:03.637
But I can tell you something,

2364
01:32:03.637 --> 01:32:05.708
when you jump into zero-degree water,

2365
01:32:05.708 --> 01:32:07.737
things are not necessarily in sync.

2366
01:32:07.737 --> 01:32:10.251
And so there's this, you're fighting

2367
01:32:10.251 --> 01:32:12.305
when you're in that type of water.

2368
01:32:12.305 --> 01:32:13.318
It's a struggle.

2369
01:32:13.318 --> 01:32:16.253
It's a, you're constantly thinking about the pain,

2370
01:32:16.253 --> 01:32:19.039
and on the side of the boat, I've got my team counting me.

2371
01:32:19.039 --> 01:32:21.059
They're giving me my stroke counts.

2372
01:32:21.059 --> 01:32:23.783
If I'm going at 30 strokes a minute, they're happy.

2373
01:32:23.783 --> 01:32:26.125
If I got up to 34, they ask me to slow down.

2374
01:32:26.125 --> 01:32:29.059
If I'm done at 25, they say, if you don't speed up,

2375
01:32:29.059 --> 01:32:31.400
you're gonna be out very, very soon.

2376
01:32:31.400 --> 01:32:33.994
So it's a constant communication with my team to be able

2377
01:32:33.994 --> 01:32:35.216
to get me through each

2378
01:32:35.216 --> 01:32:36.214
of these swims.
Mm-hmm.

2379
01:32:36.214 --> 01:32:38.645
Given that no matter how much you do it,

2380
01:32:38.645 --> 01:32:39.783
it never gets easier,

2381
01:32:39.783 --> 01:32:41.859
there's nothing that you really truly can do

2382
01:32:41.859 --> 01:32:46.684
to acclimatize yourself or normalize the experience,

2383
01:32:46.684 --> 01:32:50.184
why even train ahead of time to get ready?

2384
01:32:51.357 --> 01:32:55.009
Is that just to figure out how to communicate with your team

2385
01:32:55.009 --> 01:32:56.714
and to get all the kind of data points

2386
01:32:56.714 --> 01:32:58.756
so that they can ensure that you're safe,

2387
01:32:58.756 --> 01:33:00.777
or what was your thinking with all of those swims

2388
01:33:00.777 --> 01:33:03.202
leading up to Greenland?

2389
01:33:03.202 --> 01:33:04.569
No, no, actually the acclimatization

2390
01:33:04.569 --> 01:33:05.890
is absolutely essential.

2391
01:33:05.890 --> 01:33:09.715
If I didn't do that, no, I don't think (laughs)

2392
01:33:09.715 --> 01:33:10.637
I'd be able to survive.

2393
01:33:10.637 --> 01:33:12.196
The water is so extreme.

2394
01:33:12.196 --> 01:33:15.057
Water is also a fascinating substance, Rich.

2395
01:33:15.057 --> 01:33:19.446
So obviously between zero and 100, it's a liquid.

2396
01:33:19.446 --> 01:33:22.397
Above 100, it changes, it becomes a gas.

2397
01:33:22.397 --> 01:33:26.687
Below zero, it changes from a liquid to a solid.

2398
01:33:26.687 --> 01:33:29.020
So there's actually a tipping point

2399
01:33:29.020 --> 01:33:30.502
at which everything changes.

2400
01:33:30.502 --> 01:33:32.166
And it's the same inside you.

2401
01:33:32.166 --> 01:33:35.491
So when you're swimming in water around zero,

2402
01:33:35.491 --> 01:33:36.893
and sometimes I've done some swims

2403
01:33:36.893 --> 01:33:38.847
below zero degrees centigrade.

2404
01:33:38.847 --> 01:33:41.207
So when I swim down to the Ross Sea and when I swam

2405
01:33:41.207 --> 01:33:44.382
at the North Pole, the water was minus 1.7.

2406
01:33:44.382 --> 01:33:46.379
So saltwater gets even colder.

2407
01:33:46.379 --> 01:33:48.254
And when you're in that water,

2408
01:33:48.254 --> 01:33:52.572
it is, you are on, you're definitely (laughs) on the edge

2409
01:33:52.572 --> 01:33:54.911
of life and death, no question about that.

2410
01:33:54.911 --> 01:33:56.379
So having done the preparation,

2411
01:33:56.379 --> 01:34:01.212
having done some of the acclimatization, it helped hugely.

2412
01:34:02.301 --> 01:34:04.216
But there's only so much you can do.

2413
01:34:04.216 --> 01:34:06.070
You're not gonna be able to acclimatize

2414
01:34:06.070 --> 01:34:08.121
and make it feel comfortable swimming in the Arctic

2415
01:34:08.121 --> 01:34:09.390
day after day.

2416
01:34:09.390 --> 01:34:13.156
And at the end of the swim, so it was 14 days of swimming,

2417
01:34:13.156 --> 01:34:15.149
I remember on the last day,

2418
01:34:15.149 --> 01:34:18.336
I was just about to low myself down the ladder,

2419
01:34:18.336 --> 01:34:20.402
and I'm just wearing swimming trunks, right?

2420
01:34:20.402 --> 01:34:22.813
My team are in three or four layers of clothing,

2421
01:34:22.813 --> 01:34:24.959
and I looked at them and I was really happy

2422
01:34:24.959 --> 01:34:26.488
'cause this was the final swim

2423
01:34:26.488 --> 01:34:29.009
and I wouldn't have to wake up in the morning

2424
01:34:29.009 --> 01:34:31.337
and get back in amongst the icebergs,

2425
01:34:31.337 --> 01:34:33.340
but all of them were shivering.

2426
01:34:33.340 --> 01:34:35.441
So everybody on the boat was cold,

2427
01:34:35.441 --> 01:34:38.066
and I realized that for 14 days,

2428
01:34:38.066 --> 01:34:40.160
I had been absolutely frozen.

2429
01:34:40.160 --> 01:34:41.010
Wow!

2430
01:34:41.010 --> 01:34:42.507
You literally had ice water in your veins.

2431
01:34:42.507 --> 01:34:43.356
Yeah.

2432
01:34:43.356 --> 01:34:44.210
(Adam laughs)

2433
01:34:44.210 --> 01:34:49.210
The uniform is obviously the open water swimming uniform

2434
01:34:49.323 --> 01:34:51.792
that you've kept true to, even though you could have done

2435
01:34:51.792 --> 01:34:56.362
these extreme events and nobody, or these extreme swims,

2436
01:34:56.362 --> 01:34:57.635
and nobody would have batted an eye

2437
01:34:57.635 --> 01:35:00.127
if you were up even a three mil wetsuit

2438
01:35:00.127 --> 01:35:02.627
or a seven mil wetsuit, but you're keeping

2439
01:35:02.627 --> 01:35:05.067
to the marathon suite, you're a member

2440
01:35:05.067 --> 01:35:06.666
of the Hall of Fame of Marathon Swimming,

2441
01:35:06.666 --> 01:35:09.007
you're keeping to the marathon swimming, just a Speedo,

2442
01:35:09.007 --> 01:35:11.761
swim cap and goggles, right?

2443
01:35:11.761 --> 01:35:12.612
Sure.

2444
01:35:12.612 --> 01:35:14.043
And it's for a few reasons.

2445
01:35:14.043 --> 01:35:17.031
So clearly, I'm trying to get attention to these areas

2446
01:35:17.031 --> 01:35:20.747
and to the issue of the climate crisis,

2447
01:35:20.747 --> 01:35:22.735
and you know perfectly well that if you swim

2448
01:35:22.735 --> 01:35:24.510
across the north pole in a pair of Speedos,

2449
01:35:24.510 --> 01:35:26.543
a cap and goggles, the world's media

2450
01:35:26.543 --> 01:35:30.050
are gonna (chuckles) follow you.

2451
01:35:30.050 --> 01:35:33.077
As I was arriving at the north pole,

2452
01:35:33.077 --> 01:35:37.091
I remember I had a satellite phone on the boats.

2453
01:35:37.091 --> 01:35:39.458
There was Jay Leno was calling me,

2454
01:35:39.458 --> 01:35:42.424
lots of big names are calling me,

2455
01:35:42.424 --> 01:35:45.048
I realized that the world's eyes were on me.

2456
01:35:45.048 --> 01:35:47.810
This was the first time in human history that the north pole

2457
01:35:47.810 --> 01:35:49.864
had properly unfrozen.

2458
01:35:49.864 --> 01:35:51.743
If I was able to do a swim there to carry a very,

2459
01:35:51.743 --> 01:35:53.704
very powerful message.

2460
01:35:53.704 --> 01:35:57.444
So that's one reason why I wear just a pair of Speedos,

2461
01:35:57.444 --> 01:35:59.450
but the other one is more fundamental,

2462
01:35:59.450 --> 01:36:02.833
and that is the decisions which world leaders

2463
01:36:02.833 --> 01:36:05.000
have to make now are hard,

2464
01:36:05.862 --> 01:36:09.778
they are complex and they require courage,

2465
01:36:09.778 --> 01:36:13.174
because we're asking them to make decisions now

2466
01:36:13.174 --> 01:36:17.240
and the benefits will be felt in a few years' time.

2467
01:36:17.240 --> 01:36:19.644
And I'm urging world leaders to speak courageous

2468
01:36:19.644 --> 01:36:21.430
because if we don't do that,

2469
01:36:21.430 --> 01:36:25.360
we're gonna be in an incredibly difficult situation

2470
01:36:25.360 --> 01:36:27.166
in a few years' time.

2471
01:36:27.166 --> 01:36:28.907
And if I'm asking for them to be courageous,

2472
01:36:28.907 --> 01:36:30.628
I also felt that I should be courageous,

2473
01:36:30.628 --> 01:36:32.792
and we felt that swimming in a dry suit or a wet suit

2474
01:36:32.792 --> 01:36:33.810
wouldn't send the right message.

2475
01:36:33.810 --> 01:36:34.789
What about a swim mask?

2476
01:36:34.789 --> 01:36:37.102
Have you considered a swim mask?

2477
01:36:37.102 --> 01:36:39.181
There's a whole inside joke about this.

2478
01:36:39.181 --> 01:36:43.613
(Adam, Luis and Rich laugh)

2479
01:36:43.613 --> 01:36:45.128
I get the answer for him.

2480
01:36:45.128 --> 01:36:45.978
Yeah, see?

2481
01:36:45.978 --> 01:36:48.521
See what I'm saying, Adam?

2482
01:36:48.521 --> 01:36:51.455
Adam goes out into the ocean and he refuses to wear goggles,

2483
01:36:51.455 --> 01:36:52.492
he wears a swim mask.

2484
01:36:52.492 --> 01:36:54.984
I keep giving a shit about it 'cause I don't understand,

2485
01:36:54.984 --> 01:36:59.043
like please, put on a pair of proper goggles.

2486
01:36:59.043 --> 01:37:01.036
Your neighbor, the Octopus Teacher Guy,

2487
01:37:01.036 --> 01:37:03.506
he, I don't know how close of a neighbors you are.

2488
01:37:03.506 --> 01:37:04.339
Yes.

2489
01:37:05.244 --> 01:37:06.928
Yeah, like a-

2490
01:37:06.928 --> 01:37:08.541
You guys don't even know what it is.

2491
01:37:08.541 --> 01:37:10.381
Okay, so you got much.

2492
01:37:10.381 --> 01:37:11.736
(Adam and Rich laughing)

2493
01:37:11.736 --> 01:37:13.163
So you've got much more visibility.

2494
01:37:13.163 --> 01:37:16.421
I just wear these things just, I don't know why,

2495
01:37:16.421 --> 01:37:18.236
just it's what I've always wanted.

2496
01:37:18.236 --> 01:37:20.160
(Adam and Rich laughing)

2497
01:37:20.160 --> 01:37:21.757
Well, you've been very generous with your time,

2498
01:37:21.757 --> 01:37:24.609
but I can't let you go as we kind of wrap this up

2499
01:37:24.609 --> 01:37:26.109
without asking you

2500
01:37:28.591 --> 01:37:32.291
if you have any advice for listeners or viewers

2501
01:37:32.291 --> 01:37:34.164
who are inspired by what you've done,

2502
01:37:34.164 --> 01:37:36.280
who feel compelled to participate

2503
01:37:36.280 --> 01:37:39.096
in positive, meaningful change,

2504
01:37:39.096 --> 01:37:42.934
what is your best suggestions in terms of how people

2505
01:37:42.934 --> 01:37:47.089
can get more involved in climate change issues?

2506
01:37:47.089 --> 01:37:51.787
What is the single best thing that they can do to help?

2507
01:37:51.787 --> 01:37:52.923
A few things.

2508
01:37:52.923 --> 01:37:55.397
Please, I can't really down to one,

2509
01:37:55.397 --> 01:37:58.754
but we need to become environmentally literate.

2510
01:37:58.754 --> 01:37:59.603
We really do.

2511
01:37:59.603 --> 01:38:00.886
We need to understand the issues,

2512
01:38:00.886 --> 01:38:02.543
and wherever I go in the world,

2513
01:38:02.543 --> 01:38:05.039
even speaking to heads of state or or business leaders,

2514
01:38:05.039 --> 01:38:06.743
it becomes quite clear to me

2515
01:38:06.743 --> 01:38:09.026
that they don't understand all the environmental issues.

2516
01:38:09.026 --> 01:38:10.804
We need to become on top of them

2517
01:38:10.804 --> 01:38:13.578
like we have with, for example, pandemics

2518
01:38:13.578 --> 01:38:15.911
in the last year, year and a half.

2519
01:38:15.911 --> 01:38:19.213
I think the second thing is we are way past the time

2520
01:38:19.213 --> 01:38:21.723
when we can be quiet on this issue.

2521
01:38:21.723 --> 01:38:25.018
This is the defining issue of our lifetime.

2522
01:38:25.018 --> 01:38:27.988
And the third thing is what can ordinary people do?

2523
01:38:27.988 --> 01:38:30.853
Every single decision which we make, every single purchase

2524
01:38:30.853 --> 01:38:33.490
which we make on a daily basis is a decision

2525
01:38:33.490 --> 01:38:35.231
about our future, it's a decision about our children

2526
01:38:35.231 --> 01:38:38.101
and the whole of the animal kingdom.

2527
01:38:38.101 --> 01:38:41.555
And whether that be the clothes we wear, the food we eat,

2528
01:38:41.555 --> 01:38:44.552
how we get to school, how we get to work,

2529
01:38:44.552 --> 01:38:47.125
how we invest any money which we may have, spare money

2530
01:38:47.125 --> 01:38:51.004
which may have, every single one of those decisions,

2531
01:38:51.004 --> 01:38:53.398
it's a decision about our future,

2532
01:38:53.398 --> 01:38:56.120
and I just ask everybody to be really cognizant

2533
01:38:56.120 --> 01:38:57.820
on a daily basis of those decisions,

2534
01:38:57.820 --> 01:38:59.539
because if we always choose

2535
01:38:59.539 --> 01:39:03.044
the environmentally-friendly decision or choice,

2536
01:39:03.044 --> 01:39:06.192
then multiplied over 7.9 billion people,

2537
01:39:06.192 --> 01:39:07.806
which is the world's population,

2538
01:39:07.806 --> 01:39:10.146
that makes a really big difference.

2539
01:39:10.146 --> 01:39:11.021
Mm-hmm.

2540
01:39:11.021 --> 01:39:11.872
Yes.

2541
01:39:11.872 --> 01:39:12.898
It really is up to us.

2542
01:39:12.898 --> 01:39:13.749
Excellent.

2543
01:39:13.749 --> 01:39:15.323
Yeah, yeah, and-
The idea

2544
01:39:15.323 --> 01:39:17.793
of being self-empowered in this process.

2545
01:39:17.793 --> 01:39:19.601
And that's what's so good about us being able

2546
01:39:19.601 --> 01:39:21.345
to come together and talk about the environmental issues

2547
01:39:21.345 --> 01:39:22.811
and the climate over and over again

2548
01:39:22.811 --> 01:39:25.162
as we to build the environmental literacy.

2549
01:39:25.162 --> 01:39:28.329
So, you're so eloquent, always, Lewis,

2550
01:39:29.423 --> 01:39:32.025
and you're an incredible diplomat for the oceans.

2551
01:39:32.025 --> 01:39:35.239
It's really a pleasure to speak with you.

2552
01:39:35.239 --> 01:39:38.613
I so wanted to be one of those people huddled up

2553
01:39:38.613 --> 01:39:42.295
and shivering on the boat while you had to get in the water,

2554
01:39:42.295 --> 01:39:44.629
but this is the next best thing.

2555
01:39:44.629 --> 01:39:46.462
I wouldn't have allowed you.

2556
01:39:46.462 --> 01:39:47.316
(Adam laughs)

2557
01:39:47.316 --> 01:39:48.167
Yeah.

2558
01:39:48.167 --> 01:39:49.251
I wouldn't have allowed you, you would have had to-

2559
01:39:49.251 --> 01:39:50.297
I would have been in there.

2560
01:39:50.297 --> 01:39:52.630
Hey, you said you're done with ice swimming.

2561
01:39:52.630 --> 01:39:54.980
You said that on your Instagram, that you think you're done,

2562
01:39:54.980 --> 01:39:56.546
you think you're retired.

2563
01:39:56.546 --> 01:39:58.849
How sure are you about that?

2564
01:39:58.849 --> 01:39:59.697
Very.

2565
01:39:59.697 --> 01:40:00.647
(Adam and Rich laugh)

2566
01:40:00.647 --> 01:40:02.147
But not 100%.

2567
01:40:05.321 --> 01:40:09.888
Now, well, yeah, not 100%, but as I said,

2568
01:40:09.888 --> 01:40:13.324
I've been doing this for 15 years, and in some cases,

2569
01:40:13.324 --> 01:40:16.291
I've literally felt like a voice in the wilderness,

2570
01:40:16.291 --> 01:40:18.266
actually in the wilderness.

2571
01:40:18.266 --> 01:40:19.933
And I love swimming.

2572
01:40:21.481 --> 01:40:23.342
I want swim until the last day of my life.

2573
01:40:23.342 --> 01:40:27.734
When I'm swimming, the world feels perfect.

2574
01:40:27.734 --> 01:40:28.806
I love at the end of the day,

2575
01:40:28.806 --> 01:40:30.929
running down into the ocean and running down the beach

2576
01:40:30.929 --> 01:40:33.784
and taking off my clothes and jumping in the sea,

2577
01:40:33.784 --> 01:40:37.277
and those first 10, 20 strokes is heaven,

2578
01:40:37.277 --> 01:40:39.131
and I'll continue to swim,

2579
01:40:39.131 --> 01:40:41.693
but swimming in the polar regions,

2580
01:40:41.693 --> 01:40:43.768
which is so incredibly dangerous

2581
01:40:43.768 --> 01:40:46.807
and which is so all-consuming,

2582
01:40:46.807 --> 01:40:48.509
I think it's gonna be a new generation

2583
01:40:48.509 --> 01:40:51.798
that I hope will take up the baton and be a voice

2584
01:40:51.798 --> 01:40:55.572
for the polar regions and important they are.

2585
01:40:55.572 --> 01:40:57.393
Keep on swimming, I'll be swimming in some

2586
01:40:57.393 --> 01:40:58.731
of the warmer parts of the world.

2587
01:40:58.731 --> 01:41:02.356
I'll still be talking about overfishing, climate change,

2588
01:41:02.356 --> 01:41:04.791
plastic pollution, which is so endemic all over the world,

2589
01:41:04.791 --> 01:41:07.589
I'll keep on swimming until my last day.

2590
01:41:07.589 --> 01:41:08.886
Well, the world is certainly better

2591
01:41:08.886 --> 01:41:10.559
with you swimming in it.

2592
01:41:10.559 --> 01:41:13.691
As Adam said, you are a phenomenal champion

2593
01:41:13.691 --> 01:41:14.919
of our precious waterways,

2594
01:41:14.919 --> 01:41:18.806
and my only regret in this experience today

2595
01:41:18.806 --> 01:41:20.017
is that we couldn't do it in-person,

2596
01:41:20.017 --> 01:41:24.374
so if you find yourself in Los Angeles, open invite to come

2597
01:41:24.374 --> 01:41:26.842
to the studio, and we'll go deep and long,

2598
01:41:26.842 --> 01:41:29.184
and if I find myself in Cape town

2599
01:41:29.184 --> 01:41:31.581
or wherever else (laughs) you may be,

2600
01:41:31.581 --> 01:41:33.205
I would love to meet you in-person

2601
01:41:33.205 --> 01:41:35.595
and continue the conversation,

2602
01:41:35.595 --> 01:41:38.472
but appreciate you tuning in today, Lewis,

2603
01:41:38.472 --> 01:41:39.952
and best of luck to you.

2604
01:41:39.952 --> 01:41:41.049
Thank you both so much.

2605
01:41:41.049 --> 01:41:42.025
It's been a real honor.

2606
01:41:42.025 --> 01:41:42.874
Thank you.

2607
01:41:42.874 --> 01:41:43.721
Thank you.
Thanks.

2608
01:41:43.721 --> 01:41:44.882
(air whooshing)

2609
01:41:44.882 --> 01:41:46.823
Adam, what did you think?

2610
01:41:46.823 --> 01:41:48.886
I always love talking to Lewis.

2611
01:41:48.886 --> 01:41:50.886
He brings it every time,

2612
01:41:52.818 --> 01:41:54.278
and I always learned something from him.

2613
01:41:54.278 --> 01:41:55.151
Mm-hmm.

2614
01:41:55.151 --> 01:41:56.572
Yeah, what a beautiful human?

2615
01:41:56.572 --> 01:42:00.100
I think we can all learn quite a bit from his example.

2616
01:42:00.100 --> 01:42:03.789
And again, this is a guy who's been doing it for 30 years,

2617
01:42:03.789 --> 01:42:07.956
and to maintain that level of not only enthusiasm,

2618
01:42:09.828 --> 01:42:12.757
but integrity and commitment

2619
01:42:12.757 --> 01:42:16.041
to communicating his perspective,

2620
01:42:16.041 --> 01:42:20.381
you would think that someone who's been at it for that long

2621
01:42:20.381 --> 01:42:23.047
would become jaded, and maybe deep down he is,

2622
01:42:23.047 --> 01:42:25.411
but the manner in which he carries himself

2623
01:42:25.411 --> 01:42:29.994
as this statesman diplomat, I think is really powerful.

2624
01:42:31.753 --> 01:42:34.322
The last 18 years in polar regions,

2625
01:42:34.322 --> 01:42:36.739
like, and I love what he said

2626
01:42:37.735 --> 01:42:40.217
about he opens up the Atlas to like look

2627
01:42:40.217 --> 01:42:44.852
for like the next project, and he's doing that already.

2628
01:42:44.852 --> 01:42:47.337
Right, well, there was a map behind him on the wall

2629
01:42:47.337 --> 01:42:49.787
the whole time, I don't know if you saw that,

2630
01:42:49.787 --> 01:42:51.592
and he had that polar bear statue.

2631
01:42:51.592 --> 01:42:52.470
Yeah, he did.

2632
01:42:52.470 --> 01:42:53.318
(Rich laughing)

2633
01:42:53.318 --> 01:42:55.096
But I love what he said about environmental literacy,

2634
01:42:55.096 --> 01:42:57.691
building environmental literacy and how that's kind

2635
01:42:57.691 --> 01:42:59.630
of a responsibility we should all take on.

2636
01:42:59.630 --> 01:43:02.370
And so kudos to you for doing that, for doing that

2637
01:43:02.370 --> 01:43:07.006
for so long in relation to climate with Paul Hawken

2638
01:43:07.006 --> 01:43:09.378
and then even stuff that we do

2639
01:43:09.378 --> 01:43:12.106
when it becomes kind of part of the new cycle,

2640
01:43:12.106 --> 01:43:13.872
even when it's not, we often bring it up.

2641
01:43:13.872 --> 01:43:16.874
But then also when it comes to plant-based,

2642
01:43:16.874 --> 01:43:18.907
that's a big driver in your conversation,

2643
01:43:18.907 --> 01:43:20.826
usually around plant-based eating,

2644
01:43:20.826 --> 01:43:25.246
'cause he spoke to individual choice a major thing

2645
01:43:25.246 --> 01:43:26.097
people can do.

2646
01:43:26.097 --> 01:43:26.948
Yeah.

2647
01:43:26.948 --> 01:43:28.993
I think the environmental literacy piece is huge,

2648
01:43:28.993 --> 01:43:31.341
but the caveat that I would offer on that topic

2649
01:43:31.341 --> 01:43:35.354
is the fact that literacy without action

2650
01:43:35.354 --> 01:43:38.945
is feckless, and what I mean by that

2651
01:43:38.945 --> 01:43:41.099
is when I'm thinking about that,

2652
01:43:41.099 --> 01:43:43.051
I'm reminded of something that Paul Hawken said,

2653
01:43:43.051 --> 01:43:47.682
which is that there's no difference between someone

2654
01:43:47.682 --> 01:43:50.724
who is incredibly climate-literate and somebody

2655
01:43:50.724 --> 01:43:52.797
who is a change denier

2656
01:43:52.797 --> 01:43:54.712
if they're not actually doing anything.

2657
01:43:54.712 --> 01:43:55.563
Yeah, right.

2658
01:43:55.563 --> 01:43:56.414
Right?

2659
01:43:56.414 --> 01:43:57.930
So the important thing is finding a manner

2660
01:43:57.930 --> 01:44:00.626
in which you can plug into the solution,

2661
01:44:00.626 --> 01:44:03.967
and that thing being the thing that excites you,

2662
01:44:03.967 --> 01:44:05.754
which again, hearkens back

2663
01:44:05.754 --> 01:44:07.675
to what Paul Hawken had to say,

2664
01:44:07.675 --> 01:44:09.625
like find the thing that gets you excited in the morning.

2665
01:44:09.625 --> 01:44:12.737
There's so many things, pick up Paul's book, "Regeneration,"

2666
01:44:12.737 --> 01:44:15.554
which is rife with all manner of solutions.

2667
01:44:15.554 --> 01:44:18.862
Find the thing that excites you a little bit and determine

2668
01:44:18.862 --> 01:44:22.037
a manner in which you can participate in that.

2669
01:44:22.037 --> 01:44:25.052
And I think a way to do that is even if you don't have

2670
01:44:25.052 --> 01:44:28.211
his new book, you can go to Paul's website,

2671
01:44:28.211 --> 01:44:32.477
regeneration.org, and he's got a function on there

2672
01:44:32.477 --> 01:44:34.836
called Nexus, and if you click that,

2673
01:44:34.836 --> 01:44:38.829
I think it's regeneration.org/nexus, if you wanna go direct,

2674
01:44:38.829 --> 01:44:42.261
and that's a way to learn more about the various ways

2675
01:44:42.261 --> 01:44:44.001
in which you can participate, but yeah,

2676
01:44:44.001 --> 01:44:45.799
it begins with literacy,

2677
01:44:45.799 --> 01:44:49.609
but literacy must be followed up with the action.

2678
01:44:49.609 --> 01:44:53.906
Yeah, and I think Paul Hawken and Lewis Pugh,

2679
01:44:53.906 --> 01:44:56.354
they're kind of two sides of the same coin, right?

2680
01:44:56.354 --> 01:44:59.106
Like one is in the, one is kind

2681
01:44:59.106 --> 01:45:01.608
of doing grassroots education work and the other one

2682
01:45:01.608 --> 01:45:05.775
is doing kind of a red alert exploration, activism

2683
01:45:06.818 --> 01:45:08.626
and diplomacy at the highest levels.

2684
01:45:08.626 --> 01:45:09.478
Yeah.

2685
01:45:09.478 --> 01:45:10.900
And they've both been doing it for decades.

2686
01:45:10.900 --> 01:45:11.752
Yeah, amazing to have those guys.

2687
01:45:11.752 --> 01:45:12.585
Yeah.

2688
01:45:13.761 --> 01:45:15.080
All right.

2689
01:45:15.080 --> 01:45:17.633
So let's switch gears for a minute here,

2690
01:45:17.633 --> 01:45:20.966
in kind of thinking about what we wanted

2691
01:45:21.948 --> 01:45:24.821
to talk about today, Adam and I, as you guys may know,

2692
01:45:24.821 --> 01:45:27.716
we kind of toss back ideas throughout the week,

2693
01:45:27.716 --> 01:45:32.581
we work on an outline, and in switching gears right now,

2694
01:45:32.581 --> 01:45:33.831
we're gonna try another thing

2695
01:45:33.831 --> 01:45:36.322
that's a little bit new rather than taking something

2696
01:45:36.322 --> 01:45:39.053
from the news cycle to dive deep into.

2697
01:45:39.053 --> 01:45:42.303
I wanted to telescope out a little bit,

2698
01:45:43.292 --> 01:45:47.625
because I've been thinking a lot about this podcast,

2699
01:45:48.911 --> 01:45:52.001
what I wanna say with it and what I want it

2700
01:45:52.001 --> 01:45:55.139
to be about in general, especially since I'm coming up

2701
01:45:55.139 --> 01:45:58.389
on the ninth anniversary of doing this,

2702
01:45:59.694 --> 01:46:01.861
this is the 631st episode,

2703
01:46:03.206 --> 01:46:07.227
and if you think about that in two-hour chunks, that's what?

2704
01:46:07.227 --> 01:46:10.291
Over 1,200 hours of talking.

2705
01:46:10.291 --> 01:46:11.617
So what are we doing here?

2706
01:46:11.617 --> 01:46:14.892
People always ask me, what have you learned?

2707
01:46:14.892 --> 01:46:17.223
How does all the information get processed in your head?

2708
01:46:17.223 --> 01:46:18.443
What are the biggest takeaways?

2709
01:46:18.443 --> 01:46:20.306
And like I said,

2710
01:46:20.306 --> 01:46:22.278
I have been spending a little bit more time devoted

2711
01:46:22.278 --> 01:46:24.895
to thinking about that because I've always felt

2712
01:46:24.895 --> 01:46:27.777
like I didn't really have a very good answer.

2713
01:46:27.777 --> 01:46:32.194
And this process of self-inquiry really was catalyzed

2714
01:46:33.546 --> 01:46:36.545
or prompted by dent of my visit to Minneapolis

2715
01:46:36.545 --> 01:46:37.625
this past spring,

2716
01:46:37.625 --> 01:46:40.837
and I've told this story prior, but just to briefly recap,

2717
01:46:40.837 --> 01:46:43.822
when Black Lives Matter was erupting and Derek Chauvin

2718
01:46:43.822 --> 01:46:45.912
was on trial for murder of George Floyd and Minneapolis

2719
01:46:45.912 --> 01:46:47.806
was on the precipice of burning,

2720
01:46:47.806 --> 01:46:51.001
it was a moment in time in which everybody had opinions

2721
01:46:51.001 --> 01:46:53.078
about what was right, what was wrong, what was happening,

2722
01:46:53.078 --> 01:46:55.468
and I certainly did,

2723
01:46:55.468 --> 01:46:59.801
but we made this choice as a team to actually visit,

2724
01:47:01.827 --> 01:47:04.445
like go to the place, and rather than form

2725
01:47:04.445 --> 01:47:06.795
our takes based on other people's opinions

2726
01:47:06.795 --> 01:47:08.197
or our siloed news feeds,

2727
01:47:08.197 --> 01:47:11.457
to instead like go there and create

2728
01:47:11.457 --> 01:47:14.706
a perspective that was informed by direct experience

2729
01:47:14.706 --> 01:47:17.058
rather than the news cycle.

2730
01:47:17.058 --> 01:47:20.718
And towards that and to engage with community leaders,

2731
01:47:20.718 --> 01:47:23.843
from people on the front lines all the way

2732
01:47:23.843 --> 01:47:27.760
to the actual mayor, and that was really fueled

2733
01:47:30.031 --> 01:47:34.630
by this sense of curiosity to engage in conversation,

2734
01:47:34.630 --> 01:47:37.283
and premised upon this idea that is fundamental

2735
01:47:37.283 --> 01:47:40.758
to the podcast here, which is that conversation matters.

2736
01:47:40.758 --> 01:47:43.099
Like it really does matter.

2737
01:47:43.099 --> 01:47:44.443
And the most impactful exchange

2738
01:47:44.443 --> 01:47:46.980
that I had over the course of that trip

2739
01:47:46.980 --> 01:47:48.101
wasn't with the mayor,

2740
01:47:48.101 --> 01:47:49.541
it wasn't with the city councilman

2741
01:47:49.541 --> 01:47:52.254
or any of the civic leaders that we sat down with,

2742
01:47:52.254 --> 01:47:55.492
but instead was with this average citizen,

2743
01:47:55.492 --> 01:47:58.350
this neighborhood watch volunteer in George Floyd Square

2744
01:47:58.350 --> 01:48:03.067
who in the course of conversation on the street

2745
01:48:03.067 --> 01:48:06.203
and this person like wondering who I was and curious

2746
01:48:06.203 --> 01:48:11.054
about my motives for being in George Floyd Square asked me

2747
01:48:11.054 --> 01:48:14.869
this question that turns out was quite profound

2748
01:48:14.869 --> 01:48:17.816
and not what I was expecting, which was,

2749
01:48:17.816 --> 01:48:19.453
"What does your podcast do?"

2750
01:48:19.453 --> 01:48:22.492
He literally said, "What does your podcast do?"

2751
01:48:22.492 --> 01:48:23.378
Mm-hmm.

2752
01:48:23.378 --> 01:48:24.765
And it seems kinda funny, like usually

2753
01:48:24.765 --> 01:48:27.564
it would be like, what is your podcast,

2754
01:48:27.564 --> 01:48:29.465
or what do you talk about, or tell me about it?

2755
01:48:29.465 --> 01:48:32.532
But something about that phraseology,

2756
01:48:32.532 --> 01:48:34.169
like, what does your podcast do?

2757
01:48:34.169 --> 01:48:37.681
Really forced me to think about an answer

2758
01:48:37.681 --> 01:48:41.447
because I didn't have an answer in the moment,

2759
01:48:41.447 --> 01:48:44.951
and I still struggle with trying to answer that question,

2760
01:48:44.951 --> 01:48:49.787
but I think that I'm starting to arrive at some sense

2761
01:48:49.787 --> 01:48:52.908
of what that answer or those answers are.

2762
01:48:52.908 --> 01:48:56.009
And I think in the most general sense,

2763
01:48:56.009 --> 01:48:59.592
the podcast is like this signal for change.

2764
01:49:00.559 --> 01:49:03.334
It's a signal for the positive change that I've experienced

2765
01:49:03.334 --> 01:49:05.678
in the sense that like whatever I've experienced

2766
01:49:05.678 --> 01:49:09.068
is available to other people, like this hopefulness,

2767
01:49:09.068 --> 01:49:11.786
and the positive change that I believe we're all capable

2768
01:49:11.786 --> 01:49:14.156
of manifesting as individuals,

2769
01:49:14.156 --> 01:49:16.495
as citizens, as a global collective,

2770
01:49:16.495 --> 01:49:21.495
and thinking about that makes me think about the audience

2771
01:49:21.802 --> 01:49:25.644
and this idea of putting that question on yourselves,

2772
01:49:25.644 --> 01:49:27.985
like where all of you?

2773
01:49:27.985 --> 01:49:30.993
Like, what does your work do?

2774
01:49:30.993 --> 01:49:33.402
What does your curiosity do?

2775
01:49:33.402 --> 01:49:35.133
What does your life do?

2776
01:49:35.133 --> 01:49:36.634
Like, what do you do?

2777
01:49:36.634 --> 01:49:38.121
And I'm not talking about your job,

2778
01:49:38.121 --> 01:49:41.478
I'm talking about like you, like, what are you doing?

2779
01:49:41.478 --> 01:49:43.935
Like, can you answer that question in your own life?

2780
01:49:43.935 --> 01:49:45.596
Like, Adam, what are you doing?

2781
01:49:45.596 --> 01:49:48.018
Like, like, what are you doing?

2782
01:49:48.018 --> 01:49:48.870
Not much.

2783
01:49:48.870 --> 01:49:49.722
(Adam and Rich laughing)

2784
01:49:49.722 --> 01:49:50.918
Right?

2785
01:49:50.918 --> 01:49:53.395
Like, what is the thing, man?

2786
01:49:53.395 --> 01:49:56.853
Like, what are you up to in the broadest sense?

2787
01:49:56.853 --> 01:49:57.746
Like, listen, man,

2788
01:49:57.746 --> 01:49:59.811
you're taking up a lot oxygen, what are you doing with it?

2789
01:49:59.811 --> 01:50:01.505
Exactly.

2790
01:50:01.505 --> 01:50:02.338
I know.

2791
01:50:03.217 --> 01:50:06.682
And it's kind of like midlife crisis inspiring stuff,

2792
01:50:06.682 --> 01:50:09.372
we're treading into that territory

2793
01:50:09.372 --> 01:50:12.447
because you can overthink your life,

2794
01:50:12.447 --> 01:50:14.368
and there's something to be said

2795
01:50:14.368 --> 01:50:16.951
for doing what's on your plate,

2796
01:50:18.160 --> 01:50:21.513
like doing what's in front of you, because for me,

2797
01:50:21.513 --> 01:50:24.795
I feel like best in my life when I'm kind of on a mission

2798
01:50:24.795 --> 01:50:26.177
or whatever, but that doesn't mean

2799
01:50:26.177 --> 01:50:28.213
that's what I should be doing or am doing,

2800
01:50:28.213 --> 01:50:30.976
it's just a way to engage your mind in a way.

2801
01:50:30.976 --> 01:50:31.829
I don't know.

2802
01:50:31.829 --> 01:50:33.876
A lot of people, the yogis would say,

2803
01:50:33.876 --> 01:50:36.981
what you're doing is you're here to learn and grow

2804
01:50:36.981 --> 01:50:39.723
as a human being, like that is the deal.

2805
01:50:39.723 --> 01:50:40.863
But of course,

2806
01:50:40.863 --> 01:50:43.850
we also have all these crazy tragedies kind

2807
01:50:43.850 --> 01:50:46.810
of seemingly unfolding in real time all around us,

2808
01:50:46.810 --> 01:50:49.574
and so is that enough to like, to navel-gaze?

2809
01:50:49.574 --> 01:50:51.088
Probably not.

2810
01:50:51.088 --> 01:50:53.894
So it's a good question.

2811
01:50:53.894 --> 01:50:58.561
If you ask me, what am I doing when it comes to my work?

2812
01:51:00.746 --> 01:51:03.982
The best, I've had three times where I feel

2813
01:51:03.982 --> 01:51:06.313
like there was meaning, like what I'm doing

2814
01:51:06.313 --> 01:51:09.681
like affects the world in a positive way,

2815
01:51:09.681 --> 01:51:12.232
not during the activism, four things,

2816
01:51:12.232 --> 01:51:15.548
not necessarily through activism, one was planting trees,

2817
01:51:15.548 --> 01:51:18.777
very simple, trees matter, we need more trees.

2818
01:51:18.777 --> 01:51:21.159
I did that for a living for three years.

2819
01:51:21.159 --> 01:51:24.311
The other was when I was doing Lonely Planet stuff,

2820
01:51:24.311 --> 01:51:27.394
I hyped up this small roadside, stand

2821
01:51:29.159 --> 01:51:31.491
that like ended up becoming this huge thing

2822
01:51:31.491 --> 01:51:33.255
as Lonely Planet drives so much traffic

2823
01:51:33.255 --> 01:51:35.210
and they've made a lot of money and put their kids

2824
01:51:35.210 --> 01:51:36.969
in better schools, and that's great.

2825
01:51:36.969 --> 01:51:40.213
The third thing is the work with David Goggins,

2826
01:51:40.213 --> 01:51:42.166
and the fourth thing is this work with you.

2827
01:51:42.166 --> 01:51:45.472
And that's where, those are tangible feedback I'm getting

2828
01:51:45.472 --> 01:51:48.566
from the world or obvious, like with trees.

2829
01:51:48.566 --> 01:51:52.066
So like other than that, on a daily basis,

2830
01:51:53.095 --> 01:51:55.646
what does your life do?

2831
01:51:55.646 --> 01:51:58.196
I don't know, I take a much more Zen Taoist approach

2832
01:51:58.196 --> 01:52:00.312
and I try not to think about (laughs) that.

2833
01:52:00.312 --> 01:52:02.212
Yes, well, it's tough.

2834
01:52:02.212 --> 01:52:03.629
It's so esoteric.

2835
01:52:04.758 --> 01:52:07.117
But I think there is, and I'm somebody

2836
01:52:07.117 --> 01:52:11.165
who hasn't, likewise who hasn't been super directed in that,

2837
01:52:11.165 --> 01:52:12.634
but I think there is something to be said

2838
01:52:12.634 --> 01:52:15.514
for indulging in that thought experiment,

2839
01:52:15.514 --> 01:52:17.358
because whatever comes up,

2840
01:52:17.358 --> 01:52:20.214
we'll allow you to be a little bit more directed in terms

2841
01:52:20.214 --> 01:52:22.610
of where you focus your attention.

2842
01:52:22.610 --> 01:52:24.409
And if you were to ask me

2843
01:52:24.409 --> 01:52:27.246
even not that long ago, like, what is your podcast?

2844
01:52:27.246 --> 01:52:29.717
It's like, I have conversations with people

2845
01:52:29.717 --> 01:52:31.614
that interest me, and that's kinda it,

2846
01:52:31.614 --> 01:52:35.542
like I never really probed it any more deeply than that,

2847
01:52:35.542 --> 01:52:39.838
but anniversaries, middle-age or having been somebody

2848
01:52:39.838 --> 01:52:42.232
who's been doing this for nine years, like, you start

2849
01:52:42.232 --> 01:52:43.830
to think like, well, what are we actually doing here?

2850
01:52:43.830 --> 01:52:46.942
Like, why am I spending so much time on this?

2851
01:52:46.942 --> 01:52:50.022
Like maybe I should be a little bit more thoughtful

2852
01:52:50.022 --> 01:52:54.139
and intentional about how I'm approaching this work

2853
01:52:54.139 --> 01:52:58.532
and trying to find ways to hopefully make it more meaningful

2854
01:52:58.532 --> 01:53:01.579
for the audience rather than just indulgent.

2855
01:53:01.579 --> 01:53:04.662
And so in that process of rumination,

2856
01:53:07.445 --> 01:53:10.695
I've arrived at a collection of things,

2857
01:53:11.731 --> 01:53:13.432
right now it's six things, and that's a list

2858
01:53:13.432 --> 01:53:17.180
that may grow longer, at least I'm iterating on,

2859
01:53:17.180 --> 01:53:20.367
and that I'm kind of using as the basis for writing this.

2860
01:53:20.367 --> 01:53:24.142
The prompt was really like I'm being asked to go out

2861
01:53:24.142 --> 01:53:26.868
and do more public speaking.

2862
01:53:26.868 --> 01:53:28.627
And I have a keynote that I've delivered over the years,

2863
01:53:28.627 --> 01:53:30.758
and it's really about like my journey, it's about me,

2864
01:53:30.758 --> 01:53:33.213
it's about my story, it's about personal transformation

2865
01:53:33.213 --> 01:53:35.555
and addiction and plant-based nutrition and all of that

2866
01:53:35.555 --> 01:53:38.510
and the athletic accomplishments, but that feels

2867
01:53:38.510 --> 01:53:40.760
like expired on some level.

2868
01:53:41.930 --> 01:53:45.801
Like I understand it has value and people enjoy hearing

2869
01:53:45.801 --> 01:53:48.147
that story and it is part of who I am,

2870
01:53:48.147 --> 01:53:50.575
but I've done all of this other work since then

2871
01:53:50.575 --> 01:53:55.254
that has not been part of how I communicate with audiences

2872
01:53:55.254 --> 01:53:57.667
on stage, and I really wanted to figure out

2873
01:53:57.667 --> 01:54:01.813
how to make sense of the podcast in terms

2874
01:54:01.813 --> 01:54:06.496
of what can be conveyed in a keynote-type situation.

2875
01:54:06.496 --> 01:54:09.415
So I've been actively engaged in trying

2876
01:54:09.415 --> 01:54:13.232
to extract principles that I've learned over the years

2877
01:54:13.232 --> 01:54:16.056
doing the podcast that might be helpful to audiences

2878
01:54:16.056 --> 01:54:17.497
and making it relevant.

2879
01:54:17.497 --> 01:54:20.375
So that's kind of what motivated me to think

2880
01:54:20.375 --> 01:54:21.673
about all this kind of stuff,

2881
01:54:21.673 --> 01:54:23.294
and I thought it would be cool on "Roll On"

2882
01:54:23.294 --> 01:54:26.868
to drip these ideas out over the upcoming months,

2883
01:54:26.868 --> 01:54:27.728
like doing one at a time.

2884
01:54:27.728 --> 01:54:31.749
So the one that I wanna talk about today,

2885
01:54:31.749 --> 01:54:34.332
the focus of this is curiosity,

2886
01:54:36.376 --> 01:54:41.126
curiosity being a principle that has driven my enthusiasm

2887
01:54:43.144 --> 01:54:46.486
for doing the podcast in the first place,

2888
01:54:46.486 --> 01:54:47.891
and something that I think

2889
01:54:47.891 --> 01:54:50.961
is part and parcel of every interesting person

2890
01:54:50.961 --> 01:54:54.408
that I've ever sat across over the years,

2891
01:54:54.408 --> 01:54:59.158
and the principle really is that life expands in lockstep

2892
01:55:00.785 --> 01:55:03.236
with the extent to which you invest

2893
01:55:03.236 --> 01:55:05.829
in your own personal curiosity,

2894
01:55:05.829 --> 01:55:10.208
meaning that curiosity isn't this God-given disposition.

2895
01:55:10.208 --> 01:55:14.071
Now, he's curious, she isn't, it's not a personality trait,

2896
01:55:14.071 --> 01:55:18.668
it's more like meditation or training or education.

2897
01:55:18.668 --> 01:55:19.810
It's a practice.

2898
01:55:19.810 --> 01:55:20.884
It's a habit.

2899
01:55:20.884 --> 01:55:24.238
It's something that is abundant and something that grows

2900
01:55:24.238 --> 01:55:28.965
in proportion to your commitment to cultivating it.

2901
01:55:28.965 --> 01:55:32.321
And when you exercise it, in my experience

2902
01:55:32.321 --> 01:55:36.507
and in observing other people who invest in curiosity,

2903
01:55:36.507 --> 01:55:41.507
I have noticed that it will dramatically expand the aperture

2904
01:55:42.071 --> 01:55:44.519
of your life experience by putting you

2905
01:55:44.519 --> 01:55:49.381
in much closer contact with new people, new ideas,

2906
01:55:49.381 --> 01:55:52.998
new opportunities, adventures, practices,

2907
01:55:52.998 --> 01:55:57.415
relationships, that hold the potential to irrevocably

2908
01:55:59.057 --> 01:56:03.140
and positively alter the trajectory of your life.

2909
01:56:04.008 --> 01:56:05.036
I think what happens

2910
01:56:05.036 --> 01:56:08.453
is people confuse passion with curiosity.

2911
01:56:12.979 --> 01:56:14.242
People ask me all the time,

2912
01:56:14.242 --> 01:56:16.571
like how do I figure out what my passion is,

2913
01:56:16.571 --> 01:56:18.416
or I need to be living a life of passion?

2914
01:56:18.416 --> 01:56:19.617
You hear that all the time,

2915
01:56:19.617 --> 01:56:23.015
like pursue your life of passion.

2916
01:56:23.015 --> 01:56:25.483
But, and I think a lot of people are not connected

2917
01:56:25.483 --> 01:56:28.482
to their passion and it makes them feel bad

2918
01:56:28.482 --> 01:56:30.398
about themselves, like all these people

2919
01:56:30.398 --> 01:56:31.754
are out here pursuing their passion,

2920
01:56:31.754 --> 01:56:33.651
like, I don't even know what my passion is.

2921
01:56:33.651 --> 01:56:35.795
And you kind of feel lousy like, or guilty,

2922
01:56:35.795 --> 01:56:38.129
like I should know, and I don't.

2923
01:56:38.129 --> 01:56:39.728
Or that your job is supposed to be connected to it.

2924
01:56:39.728 --> 01:56:41.390
And I think it's, and I understand that,

2925
01:56:41.390 --> 01:56:42.510
like, I'm very empathetic to that,

2926
01:56:42.510 --> 01:56:45.702
and I've been that person for many, many years,

2927
01:56:45.702 --> 01:56:49.075
and the way out of that is to forget about all of that

2928
01:56:49.075 --> 01:56:52.318
and instead think of it in terms of curiosity.

2929
01:56:52.318 --> 01:56:55.770
Ask yourself, what is it that you're curious about?

2930
01:56:55.770 --> 01:56:57.646
And it doesn't have to be any big deal.

2931
01:56:57.646 --> 01:56:59.755
It could be some video you saw on YouTube

2932
01:56:59.755 --> 01:57:02.625
that piqued your interest in some, and prompted you

2933
01:57:02.625 --> 01:57:05.458
to like look more deeply into something.

2934
01:57:05.458 --> 01:57:08.372
So I think it's about awareness and presence of mind

2935
01:57:08.372 --> 01:57:11.849
to notice your curiosity as it naturally percolates up,

2936
01:57:11.849 --> 01:57:14.987
and then having the wherewithal to see that and say, oh,

2937
01:57:14.987 --> 01:57:17.559
there's a little spark of curiosity.

2938
01:57:17.559 --> 01:57:18.410
Yeah.

2939
01:57:18.410 --> 01:57:21.311
Maybe I should recognize that and honor that

2940
01:57:21.311 --> 01:57:22.952
by going a little bit more deeply.

2941
01:57:22.952 --> 01:57:25.293
And I think developing that habit,

2942
01:57:25.293 --> 01:57:29.882
just like a muscle that you build, will lead you to places

2943
01:57:29.882 --> 01:57:33.388
you perhaps can't really predict right now.

2944
01:57:33.388 --> 01:57:34.241
I like that idea,

2945
01:57:34.241 --> 01:57:37.720
like the idea, 'cause I think for most people,

2946
01:57:37.720 --> 01:57:42.358
curiosity is kind of like part of the firmware.

2947
01:57:42.358 --> 01:57:43.210
You know what I mean?

2948
01:57:43.210 --> 01:57:44.368
It's like, it's like in there,

2949
01:57:44.368 --> 01:57:46.300
but you don't really call it out as an individual thing,

2950
01:57:46.300 --> 01:57:47.707
and what you're doing is you're saying,

2951
01:57:47.707 --> 01:57:51.472
we all have that quality somewhere in us,

2952
01:57:51.472 --> 01:57:53.532
not everybody has exercised, like you said,

2953
01:57:53.532 --> 01:57:56.069
but even recognize it as a quality

2954
01:57:56.069 --> 01:57:59.586
that you have intrinsically and it itself does a lot, right?

2955
01:57:59.586 --> 01:58:03.788
It allows you to focus on just asking yourself the question,

2956
01:58:03.788 --> 01:58:06.933
the power that comes with that, that comes out

2957
01:58:06.933 --> 01:58:08.699
of what am I curious about?

2958
01:58:08.699 --> 01:58:10.089
Versus just kind of following

2959
01:58:10.089 --> 01:58:12.065
your curiosity unconsciously,

2960
01:58:12.065 --> 01:58:14.309
like you're saying, make it a conscious choice,

2961
01:58:14.309 --> 01:58:16.682
not an unconscious thing.

2962
01:58:16.682 --> 01:58:20.378
I do think smartphones can get in the way of it

2963
01:58:20.378 --> 01:58:23.794
like, 'cause then you're fed content.

2964
01:58:23.794 --> 01:58:24.640
Yeah.

2965
01:58:24.640 --> 01:58:26.143
Stuff pops up for you.

2966
01:58:26.143 --> 01:58:29.000
Yes, curiosity might take you left, swiping left or right,

2967
01:58:29.000 --> 01:58:31.034
that's not what you're talking about.

2968
01:58:31.034 --> 01:58:35.058
You're talking about a deeper inquiry within yourself.

2969
01:58:35.058 --> 01:58:37.845
I think that this media-saturated,

2970
01:58:37.845 --> 01:58:41.447
content-saturated environment we all live in kind

2971
01:58:41.447 --> 01:58:43.480
of is a, to what you're talking about.

2972
01:58:43.480 --> 01:58:47.789
So there would be turning off one to kind of turning on

2973
01:58:47.789 --> 01:58:50.119
the other aspect to this, I think.

2974
01:58:50.119 --> 01:58:52.167
I think that's where curiosity starts

2975
01:58:52.167 --> 01:58:56.740
to tiptoe into imagination, and they're certainly kindred,

2976
01:58:56.740 --> 01:58:58.055
but there are different things.

2977
01:58:58.055 --> 01:58:59.477
I think what you're talking about

2978
01:58:59.477 --> 01:59:01.801
is really about imagination, and you need

2979
01:59:01.801 --> 01:59:05.342
that open, quiet space to indulge that.

2980
01:59:05.342 --> 01:59:08.636
I think curiosity is about directing the imagination,

2981
01:59:08.636 --> 01:59:10.941
and I think the smartphone can be a tool.

2982
01:59:10.941 --> 01:59:13.304
It's just about your relationship to the smart, like, okay,

2983
01:59:13.304 --> 01:59:14.880
I'm curious about this,

2984
01:59:14.880 --> 01:59:17.943
the smart phone is a vehicle for learning more

2985
01:59:17.943 --> 01:59:19.664
about a particular thing.

2986
01:59:19.664 --> 01:59:23.209
It's just a question of whether you're in command of it

2987
01:59:23.209 --> 01:59:26.617
or whether it's in command of you and recognizing

2988
01:59:26.617 --> 01:59:29.794
what that relationship looks like.

2989
01:59:29.794 --> 01:59:30.647
And I just think

2990
01:59:30.647 --> 01:59:35.647
that when people say like, I don't know what my passion is,

2991
01:59:35.819 --> 01:59:38.923
it's like, I can't, I don't know how to help that person.

2992
01:59:38.923 --> 01:59:40.915
I can't tell you what your passion is,

2993
01:59:40.915 --> 01:59:43.093
but I do know that you can't find your passion

2994
01:59:43.093 --> 01:59:46.424
or your purpose or find meaning or fulfillment in life

2995
01:59:46.424 --> 01:59:50.437
without consistently applying curiosity,

2996
01:59:50.437 --> 01:59:52.364
and that just begins with those questions

2997
01:59:52.364 --> 01:59:54.750
that you're asking yourself, what are you curious about?

2998
01:59:54.750 --> 01:59:55.598
Mm-hmm.

2999
01:59:55.598 --> 01:59:57.972
And I think curiosity is something that, you're correct,

3000
01:59:57.972 --> 01:59:59.295
is innate within all of us,

3001
01:59:59.295 --> 02:00:01.308
but it needs to be called out consciously.

3002
02:00:01.308 --> 02:00:02.459
It needs to be developed.

3003
02:00:02.459 --> 02:00:07.459
It needs to be nurtured and cherished, and it begets itself.

3004
02:00:08.032 --> 02:00:11.958
Like the more you invest in it, the more curiosity you have.

3005
02:00:11.958 --> 02:00:13.546
We'll look at Davy's running journey, right?

3006
02:00:13.546 --> 02:00:16.263
Yeah, he is curious about what he can do and then look

3007
02:00:16.263 --> 02:00:18.288
what he's doing, and that opens up more questions.

3008
02:00:18.288 --> 02:00:22.442
Wait, well, if I can do a half marathon in 133,

3009
02:00:22.442 --> 02:00:24.498
how fast can I run it?

3010
02:00:24.498 --> 02:00:25.350
Right.

3011
02:00:25.350 --> 02:00:26.200
Or-

3012
02:00:26.200 --> 02:00:27.049
But behind it all,

3013
02:00:27.049 --> 02:00:30.261
what's really going on is if I train for a half marathon,

3014
02:00:30.261 --> 02:00:33.474
then I can give my excuse to buy a bunch of running shoes.

3015
02:00:33.474 --> 02:00:35.978
If I train for a marathon, then I can continue.

3016
02:00:35.978 --> 02:00:37.110
I can double down-

3017
02:00:37.110 --> 02:00:38.093
His sneaker.

3018
02:00:38.093 --> 02:00:42.291
It's the HYPEBEAST addiction rearing its head,

3019
02:00:42.291 --> 02:00:46.853
shrouded in this healthy pursuit of marathon glory.

3020
02:00:46.853 --> 02:00:47.788
(Adam laughs)

3021
02:00:47.788 --> 02:00:48.642
The sneaker-

3022
02:00:48.642 --> 02:00:50.572
Adjust, but it's true, that it's true,

3023
02:00:50.572 --> 02:00:51.852
and I think, yeah,

3024
02:00:51.852 --> 02:00:55.647
it's this idea of not needing to know the destination,

3025
02:00:55.647 --> 02:00:58.833
it's just about respecting the spark and following it.

3026
02:00:58.833 --> 02:01:01.529
And part of the fun or the allure

3027
02:01:01.529 --> 02:01:04.572
is not knowing all the answers or what the next step is,

3028
02:01:04.572 --> 02:01:07.787
and just opening yourself up to possibilities.

3029
02:01:07.787 --> 02:01:10.745
Where do you think this like connects to beginner's mind?

3030
02:01:10.745 --> 02:01:14.023
'Cause I always think people who stay young are the ones

3031
02:01:14.023 --> 02:01:15.559
that wanna learn another language

3032
02:01:15.559 --> 02:01:19.019
or do something different, or like start square one

3033
02:01:19.019 --> 02:01:21.369
in some skill, curiosity is a part of that, right?

3034
02:01:21.369 --> 02:01:22.220
Of course.

3035
02:01:22.220 --> 02:01:25.709
A perfect example would be Mary Kay doing the bike leg

3036
02:01:25.709 --> 02:01:29.046
on the marathon or the Malibu Triathlon this past week,

3037
02:01:29.046 --> 02:01:32.828
and like she's not an, she's always, Alexi

3038
02:01:32.828 --> 02:01:36.365
was explaining this really well, saying that Mary

3039
02:01:36.365 --> 02:01:40.035
is somebody who has always been the best and stays

3040
02:01:40.035 --> 02:01:41.657
with the thing that she's best at,

3041
02:01:41.657 --> 02:01:43.074
like she's just an incredible runner.

3042
02:01:43.074 --> 02:01:44.216
Right?

3043
02:01:44.216 --> 02:01:48.304
And the cycling piece has been an opportunity

3044
02:01:48.304 --> 02:01:50.920
for the first time in a very long time for this person

3045
02:01:50.920 --> 02:01:54.499
to have that, to like engage the beginner's mind,

3046
02:01:54.499 --> 02:01:56.116
to be a beginner.

3047
02:01:56.116 --> 02:02:00.458
And what I saw was somebody who was really like joyful

3048
02:02:00.458 --> 02:02:03.602
in that process, when you let go of like, I need to know

3049
02:02:03.602 --> 02:02:06.020
like, what are people thinking, or any, like,

3050
02:02:06.020 --> 02:02:08.049
let go of all of that and just allow yourself

3051
02:02:08.049 --> 02:02:10.483
to like go on this exploration of learn something new

3052
02:02:10.483 --> 02:02:12.023
to like have fun.

3053
02:02:12.023 --> 02:02:14.579
She didn't even have like a proper cycling kit.

3054
02:02:14.579 --> 02:02:16.195
Like she's an elite athlete, didn't even have

3055
02:02:16.195 --> 02:02:17.968
like a proper cycling kit, it didn't matter.

3056
02:02:17.968 --> 02:02:19.824
It was about like, we're having fun,

3057
02:02:19.824 --> 02:02:21.172
I don't know what I'm doing,

3058
02:02:21.172 --> 02:02:24.130
like I got a flat and I had to get the Tokyo golden medalist

3059
02:02:24.130 --> 02:02:26.683
to fix my bike (laughs) before I could even ride it,

3060
02:02:26.683 --> 02:02:28.465
and like, that's beautiful.

3061
02:02:28.465 --> 02:02:29.316
Yeah.

3062
02:02:29.316 --> 02:02:30.166
Right?

3063
02:02:30.166 --> 02:02:31.017
There's like a, there's a joy,

3064
02:02:31.017 --> 02:02:32.603
a release in the curiosity, right?

3065
02:02:32.603 --> 02:02:34.345
When you start to pursue.

3066
02:02:34.345 --> 02:02:35.196
Yeah.

3067
02:02:35.196 --> 02:02:36.044
Yeah.

3068
02:02:36.044 --> 02:02:39.211
So the message is to really bring that

3069
02:02:40.361 --> 02:02:43.115
into your conscious awareness, this idea of curiosity,

3070
02:02:43.115 --> 02:02:48.032
and to really be conscious of discontinuing that proclivity

3071
02:02:49.080 --> 02:02:51.571
or predisposition that so many of us have to build that wall

3072
02:02:51.571 --> 02:02:55.104
of security and comfort around yourself, right?

3073
02:02:55.104 --> 02:02:56.992
To rediscover the childlike mind

3074
02:02:56.992 --> 02:03:01.294
of the inquisitor, to ask yourself what's left to discover

3075
02:03:01.294 --> 02:03:04.794
and to really invest in discovering things

3076
02:03:06.427 --> 02:03:10.453
that you want to know, because it's curiosity

3077
02:03:10.453 --> 02:03:13.454
that really holds the answer.

3078
02:03:13.454 --> 02:03:15.788
I thought it killed the cat.

3079
02:03:15.788 --> 02:03:16.795
Yeah.

3080
02:03:16.795 --> 02:03:17.801
I don't know about that.

3081
02:03:17.801 --> 02:03:18.649
Yeah, I don't know.

3082
02:03:18.649 --> 02:03:20.062
Who came up with that?

3083
02:03:20.062 --> 02:03:20.912
Some cat hater.

3084
02:03:20.912 --> 02:03:22.039
So curious George thing to-

3085
02:03:22.039 --> 02:03:23.086
A dog person.

3086
02:03:23.086 --> 02:03:24.411
Curiosity killed the cat.

3087
02:03:24.411 --> 02:03:25.258
(Adam laughs)

3088
02:03:25.258 --> 02:03:26.110
That was-

3089
02:03:26.110 --> 02:03:28.593
Like, what a terrible message to be put

3090
02:03:28.593 --> 02:03:31.782
into some children's, young person's thing,

3091
02:03:31.782 --> 02:03:34.295
like shaming people for their curiosity?

3092
02:03:34.295 --> 02:03:36.160
Well, it was more like probably to keep people safe,

3093
02:03:36.160 --> 02:03:38.098
like, don't go out there, don't go in here.

3094
02:03:38.098 --> 02:03:41.293
Curiosity killed the cat.
Yeah.

3095
02:03:41.293 --> 02:03:43.877
That's a horrible thing to say.

3096
02:03:43.877 --> 02:03:44.951
It is horrible.

3097
02:03:44.951 --> 02:03:46.112
Don't be curious.

3098
02:03:46.112 --> 02:03:46.964
Right.

3099
02:03:46.964 --> 02:03:47.815
Be very afraid.

3100
02:03:47.815 --> 02:03:50.790
It's like we were watching "Romeo and Juliet,"

3101
02:03:50.790 --> 02:03:52.338
the Basil, version.

3102
02:03:52.338 --> 02:03:53.188
Aha.

3103
02:03:53.188 --> 02:03:54.529
And I was just thinking

3104
02:03:54.529 --> 02:03:57.794
that I could make a living on TikTok

3105
02:03:57.794 --> 02:04:00.873
just like if I could just give certain characters

3106
02:04:00.873 --> 02:04:03.883
some advice at crucial moments,

3107
02:04:03.883 --> 02:04:05.839
like all the best literature would be gone,

3108
02:04:05.839 --> 02:04:08.576
but these characters would survive.

3109
02:04:08.576 --> 02:04:09.740
So give me an example.

3110
02:04:09.740 --> 02:04:13.830
Like for instance, Juliet, you've only gone out

3111
02:04:13.830 --> 02:04:15.346
with him twice, both in the same night.

3112
02:04:15.346 --> 02:04:16.198
(Rich laughs)

3113
02:04:16.198 --> 02:04:17.652
You meet him,

3114
02:04:17.652 --> 02:04:21.197
you meet them at the fish tank in the movie, right?

3115
02:04:21.197 --> 02:04:22.982
In the play, there was no fish tank,

3116
02:04:22.982 --> 02:04:26.414
but you meet him at the party and then you have your moment

3117
02:04:26.414 --> 02:04:29.613
and then he comes later to your balcony.

3118
02:04:29.613 --> 02:04:31.040
So I'm gonna give you two dates.

3119
02:04:31.040 --> 02:04:34.046
It's probably one day, but I'll give you a two dates.

3120
02:04:34.046 --> 02:04:38.402
And then the next day, like you meet with them again

3121
02:04:38.402 --> 02:04:40.732
only for the second time or third time,

3122
02:04:40.732 --> 02:04:42.168
and you're supposed to marry the guy?

3123
02:04:42.168 --> 02:04:43.037
Right.

3124
02:04:43.037 --> 02:04:44.331
Like that's bad.

3125
02:04:44.331 --> 02:04:46.311
That's not good parenting.

3126
02:04:46.311 --> 02:04:47.461
I think this,

3127
02:04:47.461 --> 02:04:52.297
what I'm hearing here is curiosity leading you to building

3128
02:04:52.297 --> 02:04:55.907
a viral TikTok account of you breaking down

3129
02:04:55.907 --> 02:04:59.640
great literature and talking sense.

3130
02:04:59.640 --> 02:05:00.492
Poor choices.

3131
02:05:00.492 --> 02:05:03.300
Like I know we all have decided that "Romeo and Juliet"

3132
02:05:03.300 --> 02:05:05.637
is a master work of genius,

3133
02:05:05.637 --> 02:05:07.992
but let's like really break this down and analyze

3134
02:05:07.992 --> 02:05:09.858
like how prudent these choices really are.

3135
02:05:09.858 --> 02:05:10.953
Really bad choices.

3136
02:05:10.953 --> 02:05:12.097
(Rich and Adam laughing)

3137
02:05:12.097 --> 02:05:14.409
Relationship advice in Shakespeare.

3138
02:05:14.409 --> 02:05:16.328
Like I'm curious, I have this idea for all,

3139
02:05:16.328 --> 02:05:17.799
and I've been afraid to pitch you,

3140
02:05:17.799 --> 02:05:20.182
but what if we just sat around and talked

3141
02:05:20.182 --> 02:05:21.595
about our deepest regrets?

3142
02:05:21.595 --> 02:05:22.728
(Adam laughing)

3143
02:05:22.728 --> 02:05:24.225
Mm-hmm.

3144
02:05:24.225 --> 02:05:26.058
I thought that's what the show was already about.

3145
02:05:26.058 --> 02:05:27.786
(Adam laughing)

3146
02:05:27.786 --> 02:05:29.739
No, I have this idea though for my Twitter

3147
02:05:29.739 --> 02:05:31.150
'cause the Twitter is just kind of out there.

3148
02:05:31.150 --> 02:05:33.181
It's like fumbling.

3149
02:05:33.181 --> 02:05:36.131
I'm curious, I would like listeners to weigh in,

3150
02:05:36.131 --> 02:05:39.308
should I turn it in just to a reading Twitter account?

3151
02:05:39.308 --> 02:05:42.103
Like what I've read five days a week, that's it,

3152
02:05:42.103 --> 02:05:43.189
something I've read,

3153
02:05:43.189 --> 02:05:45.828
or should I just leave it as this grab bag of nothing?

3154
02:05:45.828 --> 02:05:47.125
I don't know.

3155
02:05:47.125 --> 02:05:50.658
I think you should try new things.

3156
02:05:50.658 --> 02:05:51.780
I think you should try it out.

3157
02:05:51.780 --> 02:05:52.629
Yeah.

3158
02:05:52.629 --> 02:05:53.480
All right.

3159
02:05:53.480 --> 02:05:54.329
Why not?

3160
02:05:54.329 --> 02:05:57.508
That or like some sporting news thing.

3161
02:05:57.508 --> 02:05:58.419
What should it be?

3162
02:05:58.419 --> 02:05:59.894
No, don't do that.

3163
02:05:59.894 --> 02:06:01.073
All right.

3164
02:06:01.073 --> 02:06:02.305
Curiosity killed the cat.

3165
02:06:02.305 --> 02:06:03.214
Yeah,

3166
02:06:03.214 --> 02:06:04.063
I could do that.

3167
02:06:04.063 --> 02:06:05.624
(Adam and Rich laughing)

3168
02:06:05.624 --> 02:06:06.912
I think it should just be, I think

3169
02:06:06.912 --> 02:06:08.885
it should be hot takes on Shakespeare.

3170
02:06:08.885 --> 02:06:11.791
Hot takes, poor choices

3171
02:06:11.791 --> 02:06:14.997
in our canon of great literature.

3172
02:06:14.997 --> 02:06:16.060
Yeah.

3173
02:06:16.060 --> 02:06:17.596
(Adam and Rich laughing)

3174
02:06:17.596 --> 02:06:18.799
Ah.

3175
02:06:18.799 --> 02:06:20.745
Is that what you meant by your curiosity?

3176
02:06:20.745 --> 02:06:23.076
I don't, listen, man,

3177
02:06:23.076 --> 02:06:25.569
wherever your curiosity takes you.

3178
02:06:25.569 --> 02:06:26.402
It took us to-

3179
02:06:26.402 --> 02:06:29.165
Curiosity empowers the cat.

3180
02:06:29.165 --> 02:06:32.415
We've volunteered at a marine rescue,

3181
02:06:33.542 --> 02:06:36.536
wildlife rescue up the street from you,

3182
02:06:36.536 --> 02:06:40.953
and we fed and helped clean up after beach sea lions,

3183
02:06:42.086 --> 02:06:43.507
and we did all that kind of stuff,

3184
02:06:43.507 --> 02:06:45.877
and that was based on just pure curiosity.

3185
02:06:45.877 --> 02:06:47.124
Like there's no, like we volunteered

3186
02:06:47.124 --> 02:06:49.451
for a whole season doing that,

3187
02:06:49.451 --> 02:06:52.543
was one of the best experiences I've ever had.

3188
02:06:52.543 --> 02:06:53.428
I think you're right.

3189
02:06:53.428 --> 02:06:55.750
I think there's a lot to it.

3190
02:06:55.750 --> 02:06:56.725
It's potent.

3191
02:06:56.725 --> 02:06:58.068
Yeah.

3192
02:06:58.068 --> 02:06:59.318
Good.

3193
02:06:59.318 --> 02:07:02.151
Well, there we have it, curiosity.

3194
02:07:03.958 --> 02:07:06.340
Curiosity, this is the title of this podcast,

3195
02:07:06.340 --> 02:07:08.088
"Curiosity Did Not Kill The Cat."

3196
02:07:08.088 --> 02:07:08.957
No, not yet.

3197
02:07:08.957 --> 02:07:12.999
Curiosity expanded the cat's awareness.

3198
02:07:12.999 --> 02:07:15.478
Curiosity is the cat.

3199
02:07:15.478 --> 02:07:17.054
There you go.

3200
02:07:17.054 --> 02:07:18.173
There is no cat.

3201
02:07:18.173 --> 02:07:20.532
(Adam and Rich laughing)

3202
02:07:20.532 --> 02:07:21.972
All right, we're devolving.

3203
02:07:21.972 --> 02:07:24.297
Let's get to listener questions

3204
02:07:24.297 --> 02:07:25.146
so we can get out of here.

3205
02:07:25.146 --> 02:07:25.994
Let's do it.

3206
02:07:25.994 --> 02:07:27.733
We're gonna San Francisco for the first one.

3207
02:07:27.733 --> 02:07:28.928
(air whooshing)

3208
02:07:28.928 --> 02:07:29.931
Hi, Rich and Adam.

3209
02:07:29.931 --> 02:07:31.528
This is Hadar from San Francisco.

3210
02:07:31.528 --> 02:07:33.375
Thank you for the wonderful podcast.

3211
02:07:33.375 --> 02:07:35.486
I love listening to it on long runs.

3212
02:07:35.486 --> 02:07:38.479
I'm calling to ask a question about getting into triathlon

3213
02:07:38.479 --> 02:07:41.413
or multi-sport from the swimming side.

3214
02:07:41.413 --> 02:07:43.962
I'm semiretired marathon swimmer

3215
02:07:43.962 --> 02:07:45.862
beginning to get into multi-sport,

3216
02:07:45.862 --> 02:07:48.743
and what I see around me are triathletes who get

3217
02:07:48.743 --> 02:07:52.476
into the sports as seasoned runners and bikers,

3218
02:07:52.476 --> 02:07:56.024
and my sense is that swimming is qualitatively different

3219
02:07:56.024 --> 02:07:58.071
than the two other sports.

3220
02:07:58.071 --> 02:08:00.438
And I wanted to ask if you had any advice.

3221
02:08:00.438 --> 02:08:01.465
Thank you.

3222
02:08:01.465 --> 02:08:02.319
(air whooshing)

3223
02:08:02.319 --> 02:08:03.601
Thank you for the question.

3224
02:08:03.601 --> 02:08:05.965
Yes and no, in terms of difference,

3225
02:08:05.965 --> 02:08:10.717
I think that you're in a fantastic situation in terms

3226
02:08:10.717 --> 02:08:13.074
of pivoting to multi-sport.

3227
02:08:13.074 --> 02:08:15.043
Most multi-sport athletes do not come

3228
02:08:15.043 --> 02:08:16.902
from a swimming background.

3229
02:08:16.902 --> 02:08:20.702
They certainly aren't semiretired marathon swimmers.

3230
02:08:20.702 --> 02:08:25.202
So you're leagues ahead of most people when they pivot

3231
02:08:26.679 --> 02:08:29.389
into the multi-sport arena.

3232
02:08:29.389 --> 02:08:33.506
Having that swimming background is a huge advantage

3233
02:08:33.506 --> 02:08:36.173
and fundamental to your success.

3234
02:08:37.435 --> 02:08:40.911
I think swimmers understand a couple key core things

3235
02:08:40.911 --> 02:08:43.597
that will serve them as multi-sport athletes,

3236
02:08:43.597 --> 02:08:46.512
not the least of which is they understand how to suffer.

3237
02:08:46.512 --> 02:08:50.512
If you're a swimmer who' trying to be the best swimmer

3238
02:08:50.512 --> 02:08:54.938
that you can be, you're no stranger to sensory deprivation,

3239
02:08:54.938 --> 02:08:56.356
like putting yourself in a position

3240
02:08:56.356 --> 02:08:59.806
where you're blocking out everything around you

3241
02:08:59.806 --> 02:09:01.548
and you're connecting with nothing

3242
02:09:01.548 --> 02:09:04.486
but your breath and your body movements

3243
02:09:04.486 --> 02:09:05.900
and pushing yourself.

3244
02:09:05.900 --> 02:09:10.041
So that's something that's very familiar to you

3245
02:09:10.041 --> 02:09:11.147
at this point.

3246
02:09:11.147 --> 02:09:12.745
A lot of multi-sport athletes get into it,

3247
02:09:12.745 --> 02:09:14.693
they've never really done anything hard,

3248
02:09:14.693 --> 02:09:17.091
so you have that experience under your belt.

3249
02:09:17.091 --> 02:09:19.050
You also understand and appreciate the importance

3250
02:09:19.050 --> 02:09:23.766
of technique, technique being crucial in terms of success

3251
02:09:23.766 --> 02:09:27.309
as a swimmer, and that appreciation to detail

3252
02:09:27.309 --> 02:09:30.565
in what it takes to become proficient in your stroke

3253
02:09:30.565 --> 02:09:33.620
will really serve you in terms of the intentionality

3254
02:09:33.620 --> 02:09:36.362
that you bring to learning how to properly ride a bike,

3255
02:09:36.362 --> 02:09:38.703
what your cadence looks like, your running form,

3256
02:09:38.703 --> 02:09:42.522
all of these things are already built into your DNA

3257
02:09:42.522 --> 02:09:45.325
and aren't things that you're gonna have to learn.

3258
02:09:45.325 --> 02:09:49.074
So I think swimmers make for great multi-sport athletes

3259
02:09:49.074 --> 02:09:50.729
because of their backgrounds,

3260
02:09:50.729 --> 02:09:52.486
and there's something about brain plasticity

3261
02:09:52.486 --> 02:09:55.530
that makes it so much harder for somebody later in life

3262
02:09:55.530 --> 02:09:57.420
to really understand how to swim well.

3263
02:09:57.420 --> 02:10:00.194
Like it's just, I don't even know if it's possible.

3264
02:10:00.194 --> 02:10:03.244
Like once you've reached a certain age threshold,

3265
02:10:03.244 --> 02:10:06.117
trying to teach stroke technique to older athletes

3266
02:10:06.117 --> 02:10:10.074
becomes very difficult, but cycling and running,

3267
02:10:10.074 --> 02:10:11.464
we've all run our whole lives,

3268
02:10:11.464 --> 02:10:13.551
so we already have background in that,

3269
02:10:13.551 --> 02:10:15.507
and cycling is very easy to teach,

3270
02:10:15.507 --> 02:10:18.507
and success in cycling is very much,

3271
02:10:19.676 --> 02:10:21.793
I don't wanna be too binary about it,

3272
02:10:21.793 --> 02:10:24.349
but you can learn how to ride a bike older in life

3273
02:10:24.349 --> 02:10:28.090
and you can get good at it by dent of saddle time,

3274
02:10:28.090 --> 02:10:31.506
and the technique will come along the way.

3275
02:10:31.506 --> 02:10:33.491
So having the swimming piece already locked down

3276
02:10:33.491 --> 02:10:38.050
makes your transition really smooth, I think.

3277
02:10:38.050 --> 02:10:42.252
Also because it tends to be a way to get ahead, right?

3278
02:10:42.252 --> 02:10:47.252
Like often, like if you're experiencing good at swimming,

3279
02:10:47.440 --> 02:10:50.115
you're way ahead than some of these people

3280
02:10:50.115 --> 02:10:51.577
who might then catch you.

3281
02:10:51.577 --> 02:10:52.431
(Adam laughs)

3282
02:10:52.431 --> 02:10:53.283
Sure.

3283
02:10:53.283 --> 02:10:56.523
The problem is like swimming, if proportionality-wise,

3284
02:10:56.523 --> 02:10:58.220
like swimming gets such a-

3285
02:10:58.220 --> 02:10:59.070
Small park.

3286
02:10:59.070 --> 02:11:00.466
Yeah.

3287
02:11:00.466 --> 02:11:01.555
It's like none of these races are oriented

3288
02:11:01.555 --> 02:11:04.485
around parody between the three sports.

3289
02:11:04.485 --> 02:11:05.332
No, but-

3290
02:11:05.332 --> 02:11:07.114
Swimming becomes a footnote.

3291
02:11:07.114 --> 02:11:08.620
Well, swimrun is different.

3292
02:11:08.620 --> 02:11:09.649
Yeah, swimrun is different.

3293
02:11:09.649 --> 02:11:10.505
Yeah, yeah.

3294
02:11:10.505 --> 02:11:12.975
Triathlon specifically, though,

3295
02:11:12.975 --> 02:11:15.301
the swimming is almost irrelevant.

3296
02:11:15.301 --> 02:11:16.151
(Rich laughs)

3297
02:11:16.151 --> 02:11:17.001
Yeah.

3298
02:11:17.001 --> 02:11:18.538
Ultraman is the one race where swimming

3299
02:11:18.538 --> 02:11:20.148
can kinda make a difference,

3300
02:11:20.148 --> 02:11:24.096
but for the most part, it's just about like staying

3301
02:11:24.096 --> 02:11:28.299
within range and then everything else makes up for people

3302
02:11:28.299 --> 02:11:30.694
that aren't good swimmers, and they allow you

3303
02:11:30.694 --> 02:11:33.016
to wear wetsuits in terrain

3304
02:11:33.016 --> 02:11:35.836
where I think that they should just say no wetsuits,

3305
02:11:35.836 --> 02:11:36.826
for the most part.

3306
02:11:36.826 --> 02:11:37.677
Mm-hmm.

3307
02:11:37.677 --> 02:11:39.023
So it really coddles the non-swimmer,

3308
02:11:39.023 --> 02:11:41.118
swimrun is different for sure.

3309
02:11:41.118 --> 02:11:43.197
But I'm excited about this journey,

3310
02:11:43.197 --> 02:11:47.645
and I think that if there's anything that you might need

3311
02:11:47.645 --> 02:11:49.712
to pay attention to that might be new coming

3312
02:11:49.712 --> 02:11:53.176
from a swimming background is that you have to give,

3313
02:11:53.176 --> 02:11:54.884
especially with running, you have to give your joints

3314
02:11:54.884 --> 02:11:57.705
and your ligaments time to adapt to the load,

3315
02:11:57.705 --> 02:12:01.377
swimming because you're in this suspended environment,

3316
02:12:01.377 --> 02:12:03.499
it's very easy on your joints and your ligaments,

3317
02:12:03.499 --> 02:12:05.048
so you can push yourself really hard.

3318
02:12:05.048 --> 02:12:05.897
Right.

3319
02:12:05.897 --> 02:12:08.248
You can recover quickly and you don't have to worry

3320
02:12:08.248 --> 02:12:09.889
until you get into like tendonitis for shoulders

3321
02:12:09.889 --> 02:12:13.665
and stuff like that with severe overuse injuries,

3322
02:12:13.665 --> 02:12:15.139
you don't have to concern yourself

3323
02:12:15.139 --> 02:12:17.280
with (clears throat) your knees hurting,

3324
02:12:17.280 --> 02:12:19.293
any of the kind of stuff that comes with running.

3325
02:12:19.293 --> 02:12:24.106
So you have to build your volume in running much more slowly

3326
02:12:24.106 --> 02:12:28.476
and diligently than you would have to do with swimming,

3327
02:12:28.476 --> 02:12:29.743
so paying attention to that,

3328
02:12:29.743 --> 02:12:34.205
like your lungs and your heart will acclimate and become fit

3329
02:12:34.205 --> 02:12:35.719
much more quickly

3330
02:12:35.719 --> 02:12:39.575
than like your body's infrastructure's ability

3331
02:12:39.575 --> 02:12:41.338
to adapt to that load.

3332
02:12:41.338 --> 02:12:43.419
So even if you feel fit and you wanna run longer

3333
02:12:43.419 --> 02:12:44.970
and you feel like you can,

3334
02:12:44.970 --> 02:12:47.724
you have to err on the side of caution

3335
02:12:47.724 --> 02:12:50.160
to prevent those injuries from occurring.

3336
02:12:50.160 --> 02:12:52.505
So I think exploring that and learning a little bit more

3337
02:12:52.505 --> 02:12:56.050
about what that looks like for you will save you time

3338
02:12:56.050 --> 02:12:57.742
benched from injuries,

3339
02:12:57.742 --> 02:13:00.242
but I think if you're like me,

3340
02:13:02.643 --> 02:13:05.025
you'll develop a love and appreciation

3341
02:13:05.025 --> 02:13:06.038
for these other disciplines,

3342
02:13:06.038 --> 02:13:08.556
and it will also renew your love for swimming.

3343
02:13:08.556 --> 02:13:10.892
Like when it's not just all about swimming and you get

3344
02:13:10.892 --> 02:13:12.767
to mix it up and have this variety,

3345
02:13:12.767 --> 02:13:15.127
then you look forward to your swims rather than dread them

3346
02:13:15.127 --> 02:13:17.425
like a lot of swimmers do who have been at it

3347
02:13:17.425 --> 02:13:19.058
for a very long time.

3348
02:13:19.058 --> 02:13:20.878
Great stuff.

3349
02:13:20.878 --> 02:13:21.847
We'll move on.

3350
02:13:21.847 --> 02:13:23.680
Tyler from Florida.

3351
02:13:23.680 --> 02:13:25.263
I have a Florida man for you.

3352
02:13:25.263 --> 02:13:26.112
Mm-hmm.

3353
02:13:26.112 --> 02:13:28.231
Florida man, question from Florida man.

3354
02:13:28.231 --> 02:13:29.174
(air whooshing)

3355
02:13:29.174 --> 02:13:32.147
Hey, Rich and Adam, this is Tyler from Florida.

3356
02:13:32.147 --> 02:13:35.337
So my question is a morality question.

3357
02:13:35.337 --> 02:13:38.586
I am a 31, soon to be 32-year-old,

3358
02:13:38.586 --> 02:13:41.477
and the moral topic that I am inquiring about today

3359
02:13:41.477 --> 02:13:43.017
is on money.

3360
02:13:43.017 --> 02:13:46.114
I used to believe that money was somewhat of an evil thing,

3361
02:13:46.114 --> 02:13:49.005
and I've since shifted to believe that money

3362
02:13:49.005 --> 02:13:52.896
is simply a tool, and once I amass enough of this tool,

3363
02:13:52.896 --> 02:13:56.831
then I can retire from the things I do not want to do,

3364
02:13:56.831 --> 02:13:58.133
AKA my job now,

3365
02:13:58.133 --> 02:14:01.632
which would then allow me to do things that I want to do,

3366
02:14:01.632 --> 02:14:05.855
such as work on issues, such as food sovereignty,

3367
02:14:05.855 --> 02:14:09.938
food security, climate change and the like.

3368
02:14:09.938 --> 02:14:14.396
So my question is, and I know the answer is a very nuance,

3369
02:14:14.396 --> 02:14:17.034
but would you suggest someone like myself

3370
02:14:17.034 --> 02:14:21.083
to make as much money as I quickly and ethically can

3371
02:14:21.083 --> 02:14:22.958
in the next four to five years

3372
02:14:22.958 --> 02:14:27.215
to them be able to, quote, unquote, retire from the system

3373
02:14:27.215 --> 02:14:31.052
to then devote my time solely on social justice issues

3374
02:14:31.052 --> 02:14:32.377
that matter to me?

3375
02:14:32.377 --> 02:14:35.728
Because I simply don't think I had the mental capacity

3376
02:14:35.728 --> 02:14:37.251
for both at the same time,

3377
02:14:37.251 --> 02:14:38.769
because I've seen it.

3378
02:14:38.769 --> 02:14:41.694
When I focus on social issues,

3379
02:14:41.694 --> 02:14:45.277
my job suffers, and when I focus on my job,

3380
02:14:46.264 --> 02:14:49.363
my impact on social issues suffers.

3381
02:14:49.363 --> 02:14:52.858
So, yeah, if you could help me figure out my life,

3382
02:14:52.858 --> 02:14:53.933
that would be great.

3383
02:14:53.933 --> 02:14:55.230
Thanks, Rich, thanks, Adam.

3384
02:14:55.230 --> 02:14:56.081
(air whooshing)

3385
02:14:56.081 --> 02:14:56.941
I appreciate the question.

3386
02:14:56.941 --> 02:15:00.202
I'm not sure I can help you figure out your life, Tyler,

3387
02:15:00.202 --> 02:15:01.459
but we're gonna give it a shot.

3388
02:15:01.459 --> 02:15:03.537
Let's give it a shot.

3389
02:15:03.537 --> 02:15:07.946
I think that the first thing is understanding,

3390
02:15:07.946 --> 02:15:11.334
as you astutely pointed out, this is a nuanced thing.

3391
02:15:11.334 --> 02:15:14.424
So you need to break free of this binary thinking

3392
02:15:14.424 --> 02:15:18.402
like it's either or like my job or social justice,

3393
02:15:18.402 --> 02:15:20.807
or make money or don't make money.

3394
02:15:20.807 --> 02:15:24.288
Instead, think about money as energy,

3395
02:15:24.288 --> 02:15:29.038
not a thing, and approach it from an abundance mentality,

3396
02:15:32.070 --> 02:15:35.424
in other words, not like a lack, like if I do this,

3397
02:15:35.424 --> 02:15:36.275
then I won't have money,

3398
02:15:36.275 --> 02:15:38.049
if I do this, then I'll have a lot of money,

3399
02:15:38.049 --> 02:15:40.514
but I'll save it and then I'll use it for this.

3400
02:15:40.514 --> 02:15:42.851
Like think about it, try to hold onto it

3401
02:15:42.851 --> 02:15:47.851
a little bit more lightly and in a non-binary sense,

3402
02:15:47.908 --> 02:15:52.658
and I think it will allow you to broaden your perspective

3403
02:15:54.052 --> 02:15:55.935
on your current capacity.

3404
02:15:55.935 --> 02:15:59.185
I'm not a fan of staying in a situation

3405
02:16:00.566 --> 02:16:03.516
that is not serving you to accumulate resources

3406
02:16:03.516 --> 02:16:05.158
so that you can do something later.

3407
02:16:05.158 --> 02:16:08.087
I understand there's a prudence and responsibility in that,

3408
02:16:08.087 --> 02:16:11.170
but in terms of a philosophy of life,

3409
02:16:12.349 --> 02:16:16.811
I don't think that it's a good guiding force.

3410
02:16:16.811 --> 02:16:18.753
I've seen this play out many times

3411
02:16:18.753 --> 02:16:21.091
over many years with a lot of people,

3412
02:16:21.091 --> 02:16:26.000
and generally what happens is the person becomes calcified

3413
02:16:26.000 --> 02:16:30.232
in their lifestyle and rarely if ever reaches

3414
02:16:30.232 --> 02:16:32.949
that inflection point where they're ready to retire

3415
02:16:32.949 --> 02:16:35.662
and deploy those resources that they've accumulated

3416
02:16:35.662 --> 02:16:39.329
in some kind of socially responsible manner.

3417
02:16:41.057 --> 02:16:42.268
I'm not saying it doesn't happen,

3418
02:16:42.268 --> 02:16:45.867
but it doesn't happen as frequently as you might think.

3419
02:16:45.867 --> 02:16:48.282
And as somebody who went to law school with a lot of people

3420
02:16:48.282 --> 02:16:51.346
who said, I'm gonna do the law firm thing for a year,

3421
02:16:51.346 --> 02:16:52.635
I need to pay off my loans,

3422
02:16:52.635 --> 02:16:54.957
and then I'm gonna go do the thing that inspired me

3423
02:16:54.957 --> 02:16:57.404
to go to law school in the first place,

3424
02:16:57.404 --> 02:17:01.737
I'm sure that only a tiny percentage of those people

3425
02:17:02.959 --> 02:17:05.619
have actually made good on that aspiration

3426
02:17:05.619 --> 02:17:07.440
because that's just the way that life works.

3427
02:17:07.440 --> 02:17:08.289
Right.

3428
02:17:08.289 --> 02:17:11.322
So I would caution you against that strategy,

3429
02:17:11.322 --> 02:17:14.538
and I would encourage you to explore ways

3430
02:17:14.538 --> 02:17:18.199
in which you can indulge that aspiration

3431
02:17:18.199 --> 02:17:21.970
to be more service-oriented in your life in a manner

3432
02:17:21.970 --> 02:17:25.258
that is sustainable with maintaining your current job

3433
02:17:25.258 --> 02:17:28.354
and whatever level of responsibility that demands

3434
02:17:28.354 --> 02:17:32.101
that you stay in that job for however long.

3435
02:17:32.101 --> 02:17:37.095
It doesn't mean that any work or investment of time

3436
02:17:37.095 --> 02:17:40.990
that you make in the issues that inspire you or ignite you

3437
02:17:40.990 --> 02:17:44.615
or get you excited need to be at the detriment of your job.

3438
02:17:44.615 --> 02:17:47.169
Like there's ways to do it more sustainably,

3439
02:17:47.169 --> 02:17:49.094
so alleviate that pressure on yourself.

3440
02:17:49.094 --> 02:17:52.527
It's almost like my intuition is that you need to feel

3441
02:17:52.527 --> 02:17:53.493
that pain point.

3442
02:17:53.493 --> 02:17:55.566
Like if I'm going out there and making a difference,

3443
02:17:55.566 --> 02:17:58.506
my job is gonna suffer because I need to do it

3444
02:17:58.506 --> 02:18:02.428
in this particular way that requires that my job suffer,

3445
02:18:02.428 --> 02:18:06.998
and I think there are methods of effective altruism

3446
02:18:06.998 --> 02:18:11.345
that would allow you to be engaged in productive solutions

3447
02:18:11.345 --> 02:18:13.858
without having that downstream impact on your job.

3448
02:18:13.858 --> 02:18:17.608
Like even if you gave away $100 or $20 a week

3449
02:18:18.569 --> 02:18:21.486
or a month to one of the nonprofits

3450
02:18:23.381 --> 02:18:27.117
that has been approved of in the effective altruism world,

3451
02:18:27.117 --> 02:18:29.527
you're making a huge difference without requiring any

3452
02:18:29.527 --> 02:18:30.451
of your time.

3453
02:18:30.451 --> 02:18:33.890
So it's about broadening your thinking in terms

3454
02:18:33.890 --> 02:18:37.501
of around what that work looks like while also finding ways

3455
02:18:37.501 --> 02:18:41.281
within the construct of your job to spark that curiosity

3456
02:18:41.281 --> 02:18:43.647
that you have about these issues and find ways

3457
02:18:43.647 --> 02:18:48.147
within the confines of your free time to explore them.

3458
02:18:49.381 --> 02:18:50.254
100%.

3459
02:18:50.254 --> 02:18:53.639
I don't think there's any reason to wait.

3460
02:18:53.639 --> 02:18:54.708
There's things you can do.

3461
02:18:54.708 --> 02:18:57.115
You could go to beach cleanups, you can go to a protest,

3462
02:18:57.115 --> 02:18:59.499
you can organize an event and you can organize a teaching.

3463
02:18:59.499 --> 02:19:00.714
There's things you can do on weekends

3464
02:19:00.714 --> 02:19:04.685
that wouldn't necessarily impact your job.

3465
02:19:04.685 --> 02:19:06.329
If you don't like your job

3466
02:19:06.329 --> 02:19:07.730
and you don't wanna do it anymore,

3467
02:19:07.730 --> 02:19:08.983
that's a different conversation.

3468
02:19:08.983 --> 02:19:09.825
Right.

3469
02:19:09.825 --> 02:19:11.361
And you can be putting energy towards shifting

3470
02:19:11.361 --> 02:19:12.762
your career focus.

3471
02:19:12.762 --> 02:19:13.613
Right.

3472
02:19:13.613 --> 02:19:14.464
Like you had said before,

3473
02:19:14.464 --> 02:19:18.559
like if you have responsibilities, family, mortgage,

3474
02:19:18.559 --> 02:19:21.476
you might need to create a runway for yourself

3475
02:19:21.476 --> 02:19:23.359
to be able to off-ramp,

3476
02:19:23.359 --> 02:19:24.634
but I also don't like this idea

3477
02:19:24.634 --> 02:19:26.977
that like how much money you're gonna make

3478
02:19:26.977 --> 02:19:27.977
if you do one or the other.

3479
02:19:27.977 --> 02:19:28.826
Right.

3480
02:19:28.826 --> 02:19:29.675
Like you actually don't.

3481
02:19:29.675 --> 02:19:32.021
Like if you have, say you're in the financial,

3482
02:19:32.021 --> 02:19:34.980
it sounds to me like you're in the financial world, right?

3483
02:19:34.980 --> 02:19:35.833
Sector.

3484
02:19:35.833 --> 02:19:36.683
Who knows?

3485
02:19:36.683 --> 02:19:37.781
Who knows?

3486
02:19:37.781 --> 02:19:40.100
But you might think hat that's gonna be the way

3487
02:19:40.100 --> 02:19:41.716
you'll make money,

3488
02:19:41.716 --> 02:19:44.474
but you don't know how bringing that brain

3489
02:19:44.474 --> 02:19:47.407
to the social causes and what might pop up in your mind,

3490
02:19:47.407 --> 02:19:50.322
what kind of things might come up for you creatively,

3491
02:19:50.322 --> 02:19:52.780
imagination-wise, entrepreneurial,

3492
02:19:52.780 --> 02:19:54.265
that will, you don't know.

3493
02:19:54.265 --> 02:19:55.160
No, you don't know.

3494
02:19:55.160 --> 02:19:58.318
The illusion is that there's some kind of security

3495
02:19:58.318 --> 02:20:00.434
in this certain job situation,

3496
02:20:00.434 --> 02:20:02.989
and if the pandemic has taught us anything,

3497
02:20:02.989 --> 02:20:06.641
or it's like, you don't know how long you're gonna be, yeah,

3498
02:20:06.641 --> 02:20:08.082
you don't how long you're gonna be alive.

3499
02:20:08.082 --> 02:20:08.933
Right.

3500
02:20:08.933 --> 02:20:11.670
You don't know how long that illusion

3501
02:20:11.670 --> 02:20:14.588
of security will hold itself up,

3502
02:20:14.588 --> 02:20:18.423
and this binary thinking of if I do, if I'm a do-gooder,

3503
02:20:18.423 --> 02:20:21.096
that I'll be poor, but if I seek security,

3504
02:20:21.096 --> 02:20:24.456
then I'll be financially stable, is also an illusion.

3505
02:20:24.456 --> 02:20:25.308
Right.

3506
02:20:25.308 --> 02:20:28.262
To your point, like, let's say you go, well,

3507
02:20:28.262 --> 02:20:29.989
I'm super interested in this ocean plastic problem,

3508
02:20:29.989 --> 02:20:32.506
and through the course of exploring your curiosity,

3509
02:20:32.506 --> 02:20:35.617
you meet somebody or you stumble upon an idea that leads

3510
02:20:35.617 --> 02:20:37.952
to some kind of entrepreneurial venture

3511
02:20:37.952 --> 02:20:41.959
that not only ends up cleaning out the ocean,

3512
02:20:41.959 --> 02:20:44.270
addressing this plastic problem,

3513
02:20:44.270 --> 02:20:46.052
but also makes you very wealthy in the meantime,

3514
02:20:46.052 --> 02:20:49.564
like I think it's about, I think your instinct

3515
02:20:49.564 --> 02:20:52.397
that is in the financial industry.

3516
02:20:53.719 --> 02:20:54.750
Another instinct that I have

3517
02:20:54.750 --> 02:20:58.069
is that it's about risk aversion.

3518
02:20:58.069 --> 02:20:59.747
Like, what is your relationship with risk?

3519
02:20:59.747 --> 02:21:00.597
Right?

3520
02:21:00.597 --> 02:21:02.926
And this feels like somebody who has a certain level

3521
02:21:02.926 --> 02:21:04.460
of risk aversion.

3522
02:21:04.460 --> 02:21:07.193
And I think if you address that impulse

3523
02:21:07.193 --> 02:21:09.285
or where that risk aversion is coming from,

3524
02:21:09.285 --> 02:21:12.099
you can begin to untangle the knot and look at this

3525
02:21:12.099 --> 02:21:16.016
from a broader, more nuanced, less binary view.

3526
02:21:17.028 --> 02:21:18.046
Beautiful.

3527
02:21:18.046 --> 02:21:18.900
All right.

3528
02:21:18.900 --> 02:21:19.748
Love it.

3529
02:21:19.748 --> 02:21:20.749
But it's great instincts, Tyler.

3530
02:21:20.749 --> 02:21:21.712
Thanks for the question.

3531
02:21:21.712 --> 02:21:22.573
Yeah, appreciate it.

3532
02:21:22.573 --> 02:21:24.919
Now we're closer to home.

3533
02:21:24.919 --> 02:21:25.795
Guy up in Pasadena.

3534
02:21:25.795 --> 02:21:27.435
(air whooshing)

3535
02:21:27.435 --> 02:21:28.515
Hey, Rich and Adam.

3536
02:21:28.515 --> 02:21:30.316
This is Jason from Pasadena.

3537
02:21:30.316 --> 02:21:32.259
I wanted to hear your opinions on something

3538
02:21:32.259 --> 02:21:33.636
that's been weighing on me a bit,

3539
02:21:33.636 --> 02:21:36.097
a little bit over the past couple of weeks.

3540
02:21:36.097 --> 02:21:38.227
Recently, I've been doing a lot of reflection

3541
02:21:38.227 --> 02:21:41.502
on establishing better connections with those around me

3542
02:21:41.502 --> 02:21:45.461
and spiritually with the earth and our connection to it.

3543
02:21:45.461 --> 02:21:48.771
So internally, I'm usually pretty pessimistic with a lot

3544
02:21:48.771 --> 02:21:49.974
of things, but on the surface,

3545
02:21:49.974 --> 02:21:52.992
I try to remain positive and hopeful about mine, our future

3546
02:21:52.992 --> 02:21:54.938
and the future of the planet.

3547
02:21:54.938 --> 02:21:56.709
My problem is it's getting harder and harder

3548
02:21:56.709 --> 02:21:59.863
to remain optimistic in a world that literally seems

3549
02:21:59.863 --> 02:22:01.283
to be falling apart.

3550
02:22:01.283 --> 02:22:04.986
So like right now, the Kaldor fire is scorching Tahoe,

3551
02:22:04.986 --> 02:22:06.298
and Hurricane Ida,

3552
02:22:06.298 --> 02:22:09.128
it's basically like laid waste to Louisiana,

3553
02:22:09.128 --> 02:22:12.372
and the Northeast has had just like unprecedented rainfall

3554
02:22:12.372 --> 02:22:14.105
during the summer of 2021,

3555
02:22:14.105 --> 02:22:16.338
just to mention a couple of things.

3556
02:22:16.338 --> 02:22:19.940
Usually I retreat out to the wilderness here in California,

3557
02:22:19.940 --> 02:22:22.831
but the vast majority of that is actually closed now

3558
02:22:22.831 --> 02:22:24.331
just with the threat of the wildfire.

3559
02:22:24.331 --> 02:22:26.287
So while in the past

3560
02:22:26.287 --> 02:22:28.111
it seemed like global warming was just hearing

3561
02:22:28.111 --> 02:22:30.901
about the Greenland ice sheet melting or something

3562
02:22:30.901 --> 02:22:32.782
like that, now it really feels kind

3563
02:22:32.782 --> 02:22:35.340
of like the apocalypse is upon us and the earth

3564
02:22:35.340 --> 02:22:38.655
is just raging across all parts of the globe.

3565
02:22:38.655 --> 02:22:41.106
So I guess my question is how do we remain hopeful

3566
02:22:41.106 --> 02:22:43.466
amidst this chaos while trying

3567
02:22:43.466 --> 02:22:48.120
to reestablish our innate connection with the planet?

3568
02:22:48.120 --> 02:22:49.960
Thanks again for all the podcasts.

3569
02:22:49.960 --> 02:22:52.031
It's really a bright light I look forward to every week.

3570
02:22:52.031 --> 02:22:53.030
Thanks a lot, take care.

3571
02:22:53.030 --> 02:22:54.007
(air whooshing)

3572
02:22:54.007 --> 02:22:55.232
That's a great question.

3573
02:22:55.232 --> 02:22:56.081
Mm-hmm.

3574
02:22:56.081 --> 02:22:59.722
I feel like it's a generational question.

3575
02:22:59.722 --> 02:23:03.975
I think it's something that is on all of our minds.

3576
02:23:03.975 --> 02:23:07.392
Like I have to cultivate my own optimism.

3577
02:23:08.260 --> 02:23:11.315
I think my set point is fairly pessimistic-

3578
02:23:11.315 --> 02:23:12.442
Oh, you mean generational,

3579
02:23:12.442 --> 02:23:14.217
like everyone feels this a little bit?

3580
02:23:14.217 --> 02:23:15.375
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

3581
02:23:15.375 --> 02:23:17.993
I thought you meant like millennials feel it, but no-

3582
02:23:17.993 --> 02:23:20.786
Well, I think there's a tweak on that.

3583
02:23:20.786 --> 02:23:22.620
I think there is something to be said about that,

3584
02:23:22.620 --> 02:23:25.022
like with gen Z and millennials,

3585
02:23:25.022 --> 02:23:27.752
like feeling this a little bit more,

3586
02:23:27.752 --> 02:23:31.127
and with that kind of a percolating resentment

3587
02:23:31.127 --> 02:23:33.464
about the generations that have preceded them

3588
02:23:33.464 --> 02:23:35.818
that created this crisis and this mess

3589
02:23:35.818 --> 02:23:39.720
that they're forced to contend with and charged

3590
02:23:39.720 --> 02:23:42.472
with the responsibility of cleaning it up.

3591
02:23:42.472 --> 02:23:45.624
And so I'm sympathetic and empathetic to the descent

3592
02:23:45.624 --> 02:23:50.223
into depression and hopelessness over the whole thing,

3593
02:23:50.223 --> 02:23:53.306
but hope and cultivating hopefulness,

3594
02:23:54.337 --> 02:23:58.188
I think is really important, and it's a choice, right?

3595
02:23:58.188 --> 02:24:02.605
And it's a guiding principle or kind of a litmus test

3596
02:24:03.798 --> 02:24:07.477
for how you approach everything and how you look at life.

3597
02:24:07.477 --> 02:24:11.366
And life is better when you're able to perceive it

3598
02:24:11.366 --> 02:24:13.885
through an optimistic perspective.

3599
02:24:13.885 --> 02:24:18.135
And in terms of how kind of functionally productive

3600
02:24:19.587 --> 02:24:23.178
and of service you can be, that's really calibrated,

3601
02:24:23.178 --> 02:24:25.534
I think, in lockstep with your ability to be optimistic.

3602
02:24:25.534 --> 02:24:29.395
So I think cultivating optimism and hopefulness

3603
02:24:29.395 --> 02:24:33.331
is important, understanding that change is possible.

3604
02:24:33.331 --> 02:24:36.604
Like again, that goes back to another guiding principle

3605
02:24:36.604 --> 02:24:39.979
or lightening rod of this podcast, like change is possible.

3606
02:24:39.979 --> 02:24:43.536
Like how many stories of people have I hosted over the years

3607
02:24:43.536 --> 02:24:47.313
where people have met unforeseen obstacles and overcome them

3608
02:24:47.313 --> 02:24:49.175
to transform their lives?

3609
02:24:49.175 --> 02:24:52.950
Or some issue that we thought was (clears throat) impossible

3610
02:24:52.950 --> 02:24:56.283
to navigate and we figure out solutions.

3611
02:24:57.839 --> 02:25:02.552
So I think the process for Jason begins with baby steps.

3612
02:25:02.552 --> 02:25:05.927
Like how can you participate in meaningful change

3613
02:25:05.927 --> 02:25:08.000
for yourself or for other people?

3614
02:25:08.000 --> 02:25:11.321
Or perhaps the stakes are low, but you're in a position

3615
02:25:11.321 --> 02:25:15.049
to actually do something beneficial for yourself

3616
02:25:15.049 --> 02:25:15.909
or for others.

3617
02:25:15.909 --> 02:25:18.768
And I think with each step towards something like that

3618
02:25:18.768 --> 02:25:21.374
that you take, you're able to kind of cultivate

3619
02:25:21.374 --> 02:25:23.718
that hopefulness and optimism within yourself.

3620
02:25:23.718 --> 02:25:25.409
Like make a small change for yourself,

3621
02:25:25.409 --> 02:25:27.766
make a small change for your local community,

3622
02:25:27.766 --> 02:25:29.782
go out and help another human being,

3623
02:25:29.782 --> 02:25:32.770
even if it's as simple as picking up the phone

3624
02:25:32.770 --> 02:25:35.337
and spending an hour listening to a friend

3625
02:25:35.337 --> 02:25:36.704
who's going through a hard time

3626
02:25:36.704 --> 02:25:38.674
even when you don't have a solution for that friend,

3627
02:25:38.674 --> 02:25:42.924
just being a sort of non-judgemental sounding board

3628
02:25:43.890 --> 02:25:46.969
for somebody else's pain is a huge service to do

3629
02:25:46.969 --> 02:25:48.142
for somebody else.

3630
02:25:48.142 --> 02:25:50.509
It's something that's gonna make you feel better

3631
02:25:50.509 --> 02:25:55.509
about yourself, and I think in turn, hopeful and optimistic

3632
02:25:56.009 --> 02:25:58.353
about the possibility for change when it comes

3633
02:25:58.353 --> 02:26:01.513
to trickier, more difficult problems.

3634
02:26:01.513 --> 02:26:02.834
You scale that up, of course,

3635
02:26:02.834 --> 02:26:04.509
and we're dealing with the existential threat

3636
02:26:04.509 --> 02:26:05.906
of climate change,

3637
02:26:05.906 --> 02:26:09.402
but I think mindset around how to approach these problems

3638
02:26:09.402 --> 02:26:12.916
begins in those lower-stakes environment

3639
02:26:12.916 --> 02:26:15.272
where you're creating that neuroplasticity

3640
02:26:15.272 --> 02:26:17.605
around your worldview and your perspective

3641
02:26:17.605 --> 02:26:19.936
about the possibility for positive change.

3642
02:26:19.936 --> 02:26:21.588
Mm-hmm.

3643
02:26:21.588 --> 02:26:22.468
Well said.

3644
02:26:22.468 --> 02:26:23.550
I agree with you.

3645
02:26:23.550 --> 02:26:25.386
I do think we all are feeling it.

3646
02:26:25.386 --> 02:26:29.658
It's impossible, like with the level of media saturation

3647
02:26:29.658 --> 02:26:32.454
we all live with, it's impossible not to notice these things

3648
02:26:32.454 --> 02:26:35.011
that might have other, like I think every time

3649
02:26:35.011 --> 02:26:36.530
has been a troubled time.

3650
02:26:36.530 --> 02:26:40.161
I think every time has had their generational issues

3651
02:26:40.161 --> 02:26:41.019
to contend with.

3652
02:26:41.019 --> 02:26:44.616
This one, this feels a bit more existential than most,

3653
02:26:44.616 --> 02:26:46.962
but also what makes it feel that way,

3654
02:26:46.962 --> 02:26:49.992
it is, but what makes it feel that way doubly so

3655
02:26:49.992 --> 02:26:52.294
is the amount of media we get

3656
02:26:52.294 --> 02:26:54.961
that is constantly in our heads.

3657
02:26:55.957 --> 02:26:58.327
So I understand why you feel that way.

3658
02:26:58.327 --> 02:27:01.112
I feel that way sometimes too (clears throat)

3659
02:27:01.112 --> 02:27:06.112
but there are, just because, there's, we don't always know

3660
02:27:06.214 --> 02:27:07.260
what's really happening.

3661
02:27:07.260 --> 02:27:09.697
For instance, there's someone I know

3662
02:27:09.697 --> 02:27:11.645
who has a fly fishing business

3663
02:27:11.645 --> 02:27:12.507
where I've just been connected

3664
02:27:12.507 --> 02:27:14.466
to you as fly fishing business in Tahoe,

3665
02:27:14.466 --> 02:27:16.964
and his whole business has been wiped out.

3666
02:27:16.964 --> 02:27:19.643
But it's not because Tahoe has been on fire.

3667
02:27:19.643 --> 02:27:22.610
So the Caldor fire's been on the outskirts,

3668
02:27:22.610 --> 02:27:25.719
but he's saying Tahoe's been actually beautiful,

3669
02:27:25.719 --> 02:27:28.761
and that it's, and it hasn't, like the wilderness

3670
02:27:28.761 --> 02:27:31.924
is still lovely and there's still, he is suggesting

3671
02:27:31.924 --> 02:27:36.688
that the media is wrong and it's hurting his business.

3672
02:27:36.688 --> 02:27:38.518
That doesn't mean that there aren't problems,

3673
02:27:38.518 --> 02:27:41.309
but the point is that there is, I don't even know,

3674
02:27:41.309 --> 02:27:42.442
I haven't talked to him yet,

3675
02:27:42.442 --> 02:27:43.566
but I've just been connected to him,

3676
02:27:43.566 --> 02:27:48.087
but the point I'm trying to make is within this terrordome

3677
02:27:48.087 --> 02:27:49.563
that we create in our minds,

3678
02:27:49.563 --> 02:27:53.438
we're overlooking this oasis of things are not as bad.

3679
02:27:53.438 --> 02:27:54.288
Mm-hmm.

3680
02:27:54.288 --> 02:27:55.138
You know what I mean?

3681
02:27:55.138 --> 02:27:57.759
And so somewhere, we have to start focusing on the things

3682
02:27:57.759 --> 02:27:59.003
that are positive, right?

3683
02:27:59.003 --> 02:28:00.131
That's the way forward, right?

3684
02:28:00.131 --> 02:28:02.585
If you can focus on things that are going well,

3685
02:28:02.585 --> 02:28:05.607
even as everything else seems to be dwindling,

3686
02:28:05.607 --> 02:28:08.674
that grows and your consciousness grows with that.

3687
02:28:08.674 --> 02:28:12.484
So I would say when it comes to despair,

3688
02:28:12.484 --> 02:28:14.805
focusing on beauty first

3689
02:28:14.805 --> 02:28:17.852
will help you, and focusing on things that are working

3690
02:28:17.852 --> 02:28:22.102
and synchronicity, and I think that could help you.

3691
02:28:23.638 --> 02:28:26.104
But I think you said it better than I've just-

3692
02:28:26.104 --> 02:28:27.957
Well, I think that's a good point.

3693
02:28:27.957 --> 02:28:30.124
I think it's a fair and accurate point.

3694
02:28:30.124 --> 02:28:32.473
Like the idea like, well, all these parks are closed,

3695
02:28:32.473 --> 02:28:33.567
so I'm gonna stay home,

3696
02:28:33.567 --> 02:28:35.422
but there actually are places that you could go,

3697
02:28:35.422 --> 02:28:37.454
or areas right in our backyard

3698
02:28:37.454 --> 02:28:40.694
where we, it doesn't have to be on a grand scale,

3699
02:28:40.694 --> 02:28:42.332
it can be on a small scale.

3700
02:28:42.332 --> 02:28:44.708
You just go down to the beach or go up on a hike

3701
02:28:44.708 --> 02:28:47.104
in the San Gabriel's or something like that

3702
02:28:47.104 --> 02:28:48.559
and try to connect

3703
02:28:48.559 --> 02:28:50.559
with that innate impulse

3704
02:28:54.633 --> 02:28:56.944
to be at one with nature, I think is important.

3705
02:28:56.944 --> 02:28:58.308
But I think on top of that,

3706
02:28:58.308 --> 02:29:00.889
and we talked a little bit about this earlier,

3707
02:29:00.889 --> 02:29:04.032
when I think about people who have been in this hunt

3708
02:29:04.032 --> 02:29:07.108
for a long time, the Paul Hawkens

3709
02:29:07.108 --> 02:29:10.012
and (clears throat) the Lewis Pughs,

3710
02:29:10.012 --> 02:29:11.318
these are guys who you would think

3711
02:29:11.318 --> 02:29:15.391
would be completely pessimistic at this point and hopeless

3712
02:29:15.391 --> 02:29:17.337
because they've been banging this drum (chuckles)

3713
02:29:17.337 --> 02:29:20.664
for 30, 40 years and it's only now that people

3714
02:29:20.664 --> 02:29:22.162
are even beginning to pay attention

3715
02:29:22.162 --> 02:29:24.074
to what they've been saying for a long time,

3716
02:29:24.074 --> 02:29:27.958
and yet what you find are two people who are highly engaged

3717
02:29:27.958 --> 02:29:30.461
in communicating around the problem,

3718
02:29:30.461 --> 02:29:34.388
highly engaged in solution-based discourse,

3719
02:29:34.388 --> 02:29:39.138
and more often than not, hopeful about humanity's ability

3720
02:29:40.397 --> 02:29:42.083
to finally get to a place

3721
02:29:42.083 --> 02:29:43.989
where we can overcome these problems.

3722
02:29:43.989 --> 02:29:46.896
Paul Hawken is all about solution

3723
02:29:46.896 --> 02:29:49.423
and he's so optimistic about humanity.

3724
02:29:49.423 --> 02:29:53.096
So again, in terms of trying to plug yourself

3725
02:29:53.096 --> 02:29:56.597
into some level of hopefulness around this,

3726
02:29:56.597 --> 02:30:01.597
Jason, go to regeneration.org/nexus and start exploring ways

3727
02:30:01.954 --> 02:30:06.471
in which you can participate in solution-based activism,

3728
02:30:06.471 --> 02:30:09.940
and I think that may alleviate that veneer of pessimism

3729
02:30:09.940 --> 02:30:13.770
that you're feeling and perhaps reenergize yourself

3730
02:30:13.770 --> 02:30:17.103
around the idea of being solution-based,

3731
02:30:20.144 --> 02:30:23.282
because we need everybody, we need all hands on deck

3732
02:30:23.282 --> 02:30:24.916
actively plugged in

3733
02:30:24.916 --> 02:30:29.736
to how are we gonna move forward collectively and optimistic

3734
02:30:29.736 --> 02:30:32.872
about our ability to solve the problem,

3735
02:30:32.872 --> 02:30:35.975
because the pessimism certainly isn't gonna solve it.

3736
02:30:35.975 --> 02:30:36.825
Mm-hmm.

3737
02:30:36.825 --> 02:30:37.677
Right?

3738
02:30:37.677 --> 02:30:40.986
And so we actually require you to step up

3739
02:30:40.986 --> 02:30:45.420
and shoulder the mantle of optimistic responsibility

3740
02:30:45.420 --> 02:30:46.542
in this moment,

3741
02:30:46.542 --> 02:30:49.086
and to share that with your peers and your colleagues

3742
02:30:49.086 --> 02:30:51.586
and your family and your friends.

3743
02:30:51.586 --> 02:30:55.199
There's a quote that Yogi, used to say,

3744
02:30:55.199 --> 02:30:58.669
although, I know that's (laughs) name anymore,

3745
02:30:58.669 --> 02:31:03.002
but, used to talk about it, when the pressure is on,

3746
02:31:04.650 --> 02:31:06.734
begin and it will be off.

3747
02:31:06.734 --> 02:31:09.745
And I think of that in terms of writing all the time.

3748
02:31:09.745 --> 02:31:10.769
Like when you have a deadline,

3749
02:31:10.769 --> 02:31:12.380
you've felt this with your own work deadlines,

3750
02:31:12.380 --> 02:31:14.337
when you have a deadline

3751
02:31:14.337 --> 02:31:16.665
and you just don't wanna get to it,

3752
02:31:16.665 --> 02:31:18.299
or something's stopping you or the procrastination

3753
02:31:18.299 --> 02:31:20.005
is heavier, or maybe you really still need

3754
02:31:20.005 --> 02:31:21.385
to do more research, whatever it is,

3755
02:31:21.385 --> 02:31:22.676
you don't really start right away,

3756
02:31:22.676 --> 02:31:23.860
and it just builds and builds

3757
02:31:23.860 --> 02:31:25.501
and it gets more and more uncomfortable,

3758
02:31:25.501 --> 02:31:29.901
as soon as you start, you feel so much better,

3759
02:31:29.901 --> 02:31:32.736
